var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Fitting","page":"Fitting","text":"The sessions to fit, with their data, models and settings, how the parameters of several sessions are given, and what runs after them, which are the same for every inference backend. A backend fits several sessions with a full model in three parts: a session parameter model gives or samples the parameters of every session, for the cognitive model and for the environment model, the sessions run with them, and a downstream model can use the trajectories the sessions saved. Each backend has a page of its own, with the models it makes and what a model can add for it: Fitting with Turing.jl.\n\nPages = [\"fitting.md\"]","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Sessions-and-settings","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Sessions and settings"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Session-parameter-models","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Session parameter models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Functions-that-a-session-parameter-model-can-implement","page":"Fitting","text":"A session parameter model of one's own can add methods of these. They are public, but not exported.","title":"Functions that a session parameter model can implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Preparing-a-full-model","page":"Fitting","text":"This is what an inference backend calls before it makes its full model. It is public, but not exported.","title":"Preparing a full model"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Reading-the-sessions","page":"Fitting","text":"A session is identified in three ways: by its order among the sessions, by its identifier, which is the value of its session column, or a NamedTuple of the values of several, and by its symbol name, a Symbol made from the identifier, which the chains of a fit name its entries after. These functions are public, but not exported.","title":"Reading the sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting/#Workflow-and-postprocessing","page":"Fitting","text":"What is done with an inference backend's model after it is made. Each backend adds the methods for its own models, and its page describes which models they take.","title":"Workflow and postprocessing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractDownstreamModel","page":"Fitting","text":"AbstractDownstreamModel\n\nSupertype for downstream models: the final submodel of a full model, which is run after the sessions with the trajectories they saved, such as for correlations with other kinds of data, or a regression of a measurement on a state. NoDownstreamModel is the default.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype, and for each inference backend it is used with, add a method of that backend's function for downstream models, as the backend's documentation describes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractDownstreamModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.MissingValue","page":"Fitting","text":"MissingValue()\n\nStand-in for an action or an observation that happened on a timestep but isn't in the data, and which fitting imputes, as one entry of a column or a NamedTuple, such as (; choice = MissingValue()). Whether it is an action or an observation is given by where it is.\n\nFitting imputes a missing action by sampling it from the model's action distribution on that timestep. It imputes a missing observation from what an environment model gives for the observation on that timestep: as a latent variable with that distribution, or as the value of a Deterministic. A value that isn't in the data and isn't to be imputed is UnimputedMissingValue instead. NoValue is different: it says that nothing was done or observed on the timestep. Unlike missing, arithmetic on it is an error. By default, a SessionInfo replaces missing in the data by it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.MissingValue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.MultipleSessionsInfo","page":"Fitting","text":"MultipleSessionsInfo(cognitive_model, table; observation_columns, action_columns,\n                     session_columns, <keyword arguments>)\nMultipleSessionsInfo(sessions, session_ids; table=nothing, session_rows=nothing)\n\nMultiple behavioral sessions in a generative model that are fitted together, as a vector of SessionInfo with an identifier for each, which an inference backend's models of several sessions take.\n\nThe first form splits a table into sessions. table is a NamedTuple with one vector for each column, or any other table that Tables.jl can read, such as a DataFrame or a CSV.File, whose columns are read when Tables.jl is loaded, as it is with DataFrames.jl and CSV.jl. The values of a column that stand for a missing observation or action become MissingValue, as for SessionInfo: by default those that are missing. As for SessionInfo, a session includes its environment where an observation or an action is MissingValue(), where environment_save_history saves anything or where check_stopping_criterion is true, and in an inference backend's full model that estimates the environment's parameters, unless include_environment says otherwise. The table is kept in the field table, without being copied, with the rows of each session in session_rows, so that a session parameter model can read the columns it needs, such as those that a GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters groups the sessions by, and the predictors of a HierarchicalRegression.\n\nMultipleSessionsInfo(sessions_info; cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nThe same sessions with other save histories for every one, see SessionInfo.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractTimestepModel: the cognitive model, usually an AbstractCognitiveModel.\ntable: the data of every session, in columns with one entry for each timestep, such as (; id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\"], reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0], choice = [true, false, true, true]), or a DataFrame with those columns.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nobservation_columns: the columns with the observations. When the model has only one kind of observation in its attributes, a single name, such as :outcome, is its column, whatever the observation is called. Several observations are given in a tuple, each as the name in the model's attributes when the column is named the same, or as a pair from the column's name to the model's, as in a NameMap, such as (:outcome => :reward, :cue), so that one model fits tables with other names. A single name among the actions in the model's attributes is an error, since the two keyword arguments may then be swapped. An observation or action whose value is composed from several columns is specified by how to build it from the columns, followed by the model's name: a Tuple of column names for a Tuple in each row, (:choicecol, :rtcol) => :ssm_action, a NamedTuple of column names for a NamedTuple, (; choice = :choicecol, rt = :rtcol) => :ssm_action, or a Tuple of column names and a function of their values, which needs parentheses around it, (:choicecol, :rtcol) => ((choice, rt) -> [choice, rt]) => :ssm_action. When the model has only one kind of them in its attributes, the name can be left out of the last two, as in (; choice = :choicecol, rt = :rtcol). A missing value in any of the columns makes the whole value missing.\naction_columns: the columns with the actions, in the same forms.\nsession_columns: the column or columns which separate sessions, such as :id or (:id, :block). A session is every row with one combination of their values, in the order of the first row of each. The identifier of a session is its value in a single column, or a NamedTuple of its values in several.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, as one size for all, or the name of a column with the size of each timestep.\nmissing_observation_values=(missing,), missing_action_values=(missing,), impute_missing_observations=true and impute_missing_actions=true: the values in the columns which stand for a missing observation or action, and whether those are imputed, as for SessionInfo. They are marked in the whole table before it is split, so that the columns of every session have the same element types.\nThe other keyword arguments of SessionInfo, which every session gets.\n\nThe second form takes sessions that are already prepared. They have no table, unless it is given as table, with the rows of each session in it as session_rows, a vector of vectors of row numbers. Each argument is kept in a field of the same name.\n\nEach session is identified in three ways: by its order, its index in sessions, which the models of several sessions number it by and CognitiveModeling.session_index finds; by its identifier, the value of its session column, or a NamedTuple of the values when there are several, as in session_ids; and by its symbol name, a Symbol made from the identifier by CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol, such as S1 or id_S1__block_2, which the chains of a fit name its entries after.\n\nIn the REPL, the sessions show a summary on one line each: how many there are, and the models and the names in the data of the first.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Counter <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::Counter) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :probability, states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> table = (; id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\", \"S2\"], outcome = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.0], choice = [true, false, true, missing, false]);\n\njulia> sessions_info = MultipleSessionsInfo(Counter(), table; observation_columns = :outcome => :reward, action_columns = :choice, session_columns = :id, who_starts = EnvironmentStarts())\nMultipleSessionsInfo\nSessions: 2\nCognitive model: Counter\nEnvironment: none\nObservations: reward\nActions: choice\n\njulia> sessions_info.session_ids\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"S1\"\n \"S2\"\n\njulia> sessions_info.sessions[2].actions\n(choice = Union{MissingValue, Bool}[true, MissingValue(), false],)\n\nA DataFrame is given in the same way:\n\njulia> using DataFrames\n\njulia> data = DataFrame(id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\"], reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0], choice = [true, missing, false, true]);\n\njulia> sessions_info = MultipleSessionsInfo(Counter(), data; observation_columns = :reward, action_columns = :choice, session_columns = :id, who_starts = EnvironmentStarts());\n\njulia> sessions_info.sessions[1].actions\n(choice = Union{MissingValue, Bool}[true, MissingValue()],)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.MultipleSessionsInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.NoDownstreamModel","page":"Fitting","text":"NoDownstreamModel()\n\nThe downstream_model of a full model that has no third part, so that nothing runs after the sessions. This is the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoDownstreamModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.NoEnvironment","page":"Fitting","text":"NoEnvironment()\n\nThe environment of a session that has none, which is the default of SessionInfo. Every observation then comes from the data, and none can be MissingValue, since nothing imputes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoEnvironment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.ParallelSessions","page":"Fitting","text":"ParallelSessions()\n\nGiven as parallelization to an inference backend's model of several sessions, it spreads the sessions over the threads Julia was started with, as Threads.@threads does. What the model then needs, which automatic differentiation packages work with it and how much faster it is are described in the documentation of each inference backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ParallelSessions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.SerialSessions","page":"Fitting","text":"SerialSessions()\n\nThe default parallelization of an inference backend's models of several sessions: the sessions run one after the other. See ParallelSessions for the other choice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SerialSessions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.SessionInfo","page":"Fitting","text":"SessionInfo(cognitive_model, observations, actions; who_starts, <keyword arguments>)\n\nEverything about one behavioral session that is fixed within a generative model to be fitted: the data, the models and the settings, checked and prepared once. An inference backend's model of the session takes it together with the parameters, which are what changes between evaluations. MultipleSessionsInfo makes one for each session in a table.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractTimestepModel: the cognitive model, usually an AbstractCognitiveModel.\nobservations: the observations in the data, as a NamedTuple with one vector for each name and one entry for each timestep, such as (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0]). The data must have every observation in the model's attributes, from attribute_names, and can have more. When the model has only one kind of observation in its attributes, a single vector, [1.0, 0.0, 1.0], is the data of that observation, whatever its name. An observation that wasn't recorded is MissingValue, which environment imputes unless impute_missing_observations is false, and one that wasn't made is NoValue.\nactions: the actions in the data, in the same forms, with exactly the actions in the model's attributes. An action that was taken but not recorded is MissingValue(), which is imputed unless impute_missing_actions is false, and an action that wasn't taken is NoValue(). Neither is scored.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: whether the model acts on the observation of the same timestep, EnvironmentStarts(), or on the observation of the timestep before, AgentStarts(), as in simulate. It has no default, because a wrong choice shifts the model by a timestep without any error.\nenvironment::Union{NoEnvironment, AbstractTimestepModel}=NoEnvironment(): an environment model, usually an AbstractEnvironmentModel, which imputes the observations that are MissingValue(), and against whose distributions the observations in the data are scored. Its outputs must be the inputs of cognitive_model and the other way round, directly or through observation_map and action_map. Without it, the data can't have observations that are MissingValue(). Whether the session includes it is decided by include_environment, and it is kept in the session either way.\ninclude_environment::Union{Nothing, Bool}=nothing: whether the session includes environment, so that it runs on every timestep. By default, with nothing, it is included where an observation or an action is MissingValue(), where environment_save_history saves anything, or where check_stopping_criterion is true: the scored observations then inform what is imputed, such as which arm of a bandit a missing choice was, from the reward that followed. It is also included in every session of an inference backend's full model that estimates the environment's parameters, so that the observations inform them. Otherwise it is left out, since the scores of its observations would be the same for every value of the parameters, and the session has none of its variables. With true it is always included, such as for a model of one's own which estimates its parameters. With false it never is: an observation that is MissingValue(), a selection in environment_save_history and check_stopping_criterion = true are then errors, and an action that is MissingValue() is imputed without the observations informing it.\nobservation_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing and action_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which observations of environment the cognitive model receives, and which of its actions the environment receives, and under which names, as NameMaps, as for simulate. The data is in the cognitive model's names, and is scored against the environment's distributions as the map passes them on. An output of the environment that the map doesn't pass on isn't in the data, so it is imputed on every timestep. Each model's history is in its own names.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, as in simulate.\ncognitive_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): which states, inputs, outputs and output distributions of cognitive_model a fitted session returns, by name, and whether timestep 0 is, as a SaveHistory in the model's own names. Nothing is selected by default. What is returned names them by their role, as states, observations, actions and action_distributions, as simulate does.\nenvironment_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): the same for environment, whose inputs are the actions and whose outputs are the observations, returned under environment. Selecting anything makes the environment run in the session, also when nothing is missing, which adds its scores of the observations to the log density; with fixed environment parameters they are the same for every value of the cognitive parameters, so the posterior is unchanged. Whether timestep 0 is saved follows cognitive_save_history.\ncheck_stopping_criterion::Union{Nothing, Bool}=nothing: when the stopping_criterion of the two models is checked before a timestep, so that the session ends there, with what it saved up to it. With nothing, it is checked on the timesteps after the last one with data. With true, it is checked before every timestep, and a criterion that is met while the data continues is an error. With false, it is never checked. The extended help says why.\nmissing_observation_values=(missing,) and missing_action_values=(missing,): the values in the data which stand for a missing observation or action, as one value, such as -1, or several in a tuple or a vector, such as (missing, -1). Each is replaced by MissingValue(). A value is compared to them with isequal, and () keeps every value as it is, missing too. MissingValue() can also be put in the data directly.\nimpute_missing_observations::Bool=true and impute_missing_actions::Bool=true: whether the observations and the actions that are MissingValue() are imputed, as latent variables. With false, nothing is sampled for them, and they are replaced by UnimputedMissingValue here, which the models receive as it is.\nprevious_observation and previous_action: what the first timestep receives as the observation and the action before it, in the cognitive model's names, with BeforeFirstTimestep for each name by default, as in simulate. The environment starts from the same observation in its own names, with BeforeFirstTimestep() for an output that observation_map doesn't pass on. The states where the session starts are not given here, since they come from initialize_state with the parameters that are fitted.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, except the values that stand for a missing one and whether they are imputed, whose work is done in the data here. The other fields are includes_environment, whether the session includes the environment, as include_environment decides it, environment_observations, the data of the environment's outputs in its own names, previous_environment_observation, the observation the environment starts from in its own names, actions_to_condition_on and observations_to_condition_on, the values in the data of each name, which fitting conditions on, with the observations in the environment's names, n_timesteps, saved_timestep_sizes, the sizes a fitted session returns with what it saves, and first_timestep_to_check_stopping, the first timestep before which the stopping criterion is checked, as check_stopping_criterion decides it.\n\nSessionInfo(session_info; cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nUpdate a session to have other save histories, which returns a new SessionInfo. This is for after a fit: a session is fitted fastest when it saves nothing, and its states, or anything else it can save, are read afterwards from the updated session, by evaluating its model with each sample of the fit. The two models have the same variables, so every sample fits both. The documentation of each inference backend describes how.\n\nIn the REPL, a session shows a summary on one line each: its models, its number of timesteps, the names of the observations and the actions in its data, which are the cognitive model's names, and who starts.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Counter <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::Counter) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :probability, states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> session_info = SessionInfo(Counter(), (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, missing]), (; choice = [true, missing, false]); who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), impute_missing_observations = false)\nSessionInfo\nCognitive model: Counter\nEnvironment: none\nTimesteps: 3\nObservations: reward\nActions: choice\nWho starts: environment\n\njulia> session_info.observations.reward[3], session_info.actions.choice[2]\n(UnimputedMissingValue(), MissingValue())\n\njulia> session_info.actions_to_condition_on\n(choice = Bool[1, 0],)\n\nExtended help\n\nWhen the stopping criterion is checked follows from the data. The last timestep with data is the last one where an observation or an action isn't MissingValue(). With check_stopping_criterion = nothing, the criterion is only checked after it, so that a fit, where every timestep has data, never checks it, and a session prepared by prepare_for_prediction, whose data is all MissingValue(), ends as a simulation does. With true, the session includes its environment, whose criterion is checked too. A criterion that is met while the data continues is then an error, since the values in the data after that timestep couldn't be scored, which checks that the models agree with the length of the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SessionInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.UnimputedMissingValue","page":"Fitting","text":"UnimputedMissingValue()\n\nStand-in for an action or an observation that happened on a timestep but isn't in the data, and which fitting doesn't impute, as one entry of a column or a NamedTuple, such as (; reward = UnimputedMissingValue()).\n\nNothing is sampled or scored for it, and the models receive it as it is: a cognitive model as its observation, or as its previous action on the next timestep, and an environment model as the action it responds to. A model which is fitted to such data must handle it, as it does the other subtypes of AbstractNoValue. Useful for a session without an environment model whose data lacks some observations, and for missing actions which shouldn't become latent variables. A SessionInfo puts it in place of the missing observations with impute_missing_observations = false, and of the missing actions with impute_missing_actions = false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.UnimputedMissingValue"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_for_prediction-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Fitting","text":"prepare_for_prediction(session_info; check_stopping_criterion=nothing)\nprepare_for_prediction(sessions_info; check_stopping_criterion=nothing)\n\nReturn a copy of a SessionInfo, or of every session in a MultipleSessionsInfo, where every action is MissingValue, and every observation too when the session has an environment model that it can include, so that an inference backend's model of the copy simulates them instead of fitting to them. This can be used for predictive checks: evaluating the model of the copy with the samples of a fit to the sessions, or sampling it from its prior, simulates each session again, with the same number of timesteps at most, the same settings and the same environment. The observations of a session without an environment stay those in the data, as for an experiment whose observations are fixed in advance. Every action is simulated, whatever it was in the data, also one that was UnimputedMissingValue or NoValue. The copy checks the stopping_criterion of the models as check_stopping_criterion says, as for SessionInfo, whatever the session was prepared with: by default after the last timestep with data, which in a session with an environment is before every timestep, so that the copy can end before the data did, as a simulation does.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Counter <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::Counter) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :probability, states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> session_info = SessionInfo(Counter(), [1.0, 0.0, 1.0], [true, false, true]; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts());\n\njulia> prepare_for_prediction(session_info).actions\n(choice = [MissingValue(), MissingValue(), MissingValue()],)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_for_prediction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractSessionParametersModel","page":"Fitting","text":"AbstractSessionParametersModel\n\nSupertype for session parameter models: the part of a full model, the model that an inference backend fits to several sessions, which gives or estimates the parameters of every session, for the cognitive model and for the environment model alike. The package comes with NoParameters, FixedSessionParameters, FixedParametersBySession, IdenticalSessionParameters, IndependentSessionParameters, GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters and HierarchicalRegression, and CompositeSessionParameters combines several, each giving some of the parameters.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype, and for each inference backend it is used with, add a method of that backend's function for session parameter models, as the backend's documentation describes. A method of CognitiveModeling.parameter_names lets the parameters it gives be checked before a fit, and a method of CognitiveModeling.prepare_parameters_model lets it work out once, before the backend's model is made, what doesn't change between evaluations. A model which only gives values, and estimates nothing, adds a method of CognitiveModeling.estimates_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractSessionParametersModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.NoParameters","page":"Fitting","text":"NoParameters()\n\nThe session parameter model of a full model which gives no parameters, so that each parameter takes the value the model gives it in default_parameters. It is the default for the parameters of the environment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.FixedSessionParameters","page":"Fitting","text":"FixedSessionParameters(parameters)\n\nThe session parameter model of a full model which gives every session the same values, such as (; scale = 2.0), with nothing sampled. The values are kept in the field parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.FixedSessionParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.FixedParametersBySession","page":"Fitting","text":"FixedParametersBySession(parameters_by_session)\n\nThe session parameter model of a full model which gives each session parameters of its own, with nothing sampled. parameters_by_session holds the parameters of each session in one of three forms: by the session's symbol name, in a NamedTuple with an entry for each session, such as (; S1 = (; scale = 2.0), S2 = (; scale = 3.0)); by its identifier, in a dictionary, such as Dict(\"S1\" => (; scale = 2.0), \"S2\" => (; scale = 3.0)); or in the order of the sessions, as a NamedTuple with one vector for each parameter, such as (; scale = [2.0, 3.0]). A session with several session columns has an identifier like (; id = \"S1\", block = 2) and a symbol name like id_S1__block_2, as CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol gives it. Every session must have the same parameters. A full model makes the first two forms into the third once, before it is made. The parameters are kept in the field parameters_by_session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.FixedParametersBySession"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.IdenticalSessionParameters","page":"Fitting","text":"IdenticalSessionParameters(priors)\n\nThe session parameter model of a full model which samples each parameter once, from its prior, and gives every session that value. priors is a NamedTuple with the name of each parameter that is fitted and the distribution it is sampled from, such as (; learning_rate = Beta(2, 2)). The priors are kept in the field priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.IdenticalSessionParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.IndependentSessionParameters","page":"Fitting","text":"IndependentSessionParameters(priors)\n\nThe session parameter model of a full model which samples each parameter for every session from the same prior, with nothing shared between the sessions. priors is a NamedTuple with the name of each parameter that is fitted and the distribution it is sampled from, such as (; learning_rate = Beta(2, 2), inverse_temperature = LogNormal(0, 1)). The priors are kept in the field priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.IndependentSessionParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters","page":"Fitting","text":"GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters(parameters)\n\nThe session parameter model of a full model which samples each parameter of every session from a group-level distribution, whose own parameters are sampled from priors, once for each group of sessions. parameters is a NamedTuple with a HierarchicalParameter for each parameter that is fitted, which has its priors, its group-level distribution, its transformation and the columns that group the sessions, so the parameters can be grouped by different columns. It is kept in the field parameters.\n\nA full model finds the group of every session once, before it is made, from the table that MultipleSessionsInfo keeps, so sessions that were prepared one at a time can only be in one group. The field groups is nothing until then, and afterwards has, for each parameter, the group of every session, group_of_each_session, the number of groups, n_groups, and the symbol name of each group, group_symbol_names.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Turing\nusing LogExpFunctions: logistic\n\nsession_parameter_model = GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters((;\n    learning_rate = HierarchicalParameter(\n        (; mean = Normal(0, 1), sd = Exponential(1));\n        transformation = logistic,\n        group_by = :diagnosis,\n    ),\n    inverse_temperature = HierarchicalParameter(\n        (; mean = Normal(0, 1), sd = Exponential(1)); transformation = exp\n    ),\n))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.HierarchicalParameter","page":"Fitting","text":"HierarchicalParameter(priors; distribution=nothing, transformation=identity,\n                      group_by=nothing, centered=nothing)\n\nHow one parameter of a GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters is sampled for every session: from a group-level distribution, whose own parameters are sampled from priors, once for each group of sessions.\n\nArguments\n\npriors::NamedTuple: the priors of the parameters of the group-level distribution, in the order in which the distribution takes them, such as (; mean = Normal(0, 1), sd = Exponential(1)). Their names name the variables of the group-level parameters.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ndistribution=nothing: the group-level distribution, as a function of its parameters, such as Normal or (location, scale) -> Laplace(location, scale). nothing is a normal distribution, whose two priors are those of its mean and its standard deviation.\ntransformation=identity: a function applied to the value of each session after it is sampled, such as exp for a parameter that must be positive or logistic for one between 0 and 1, as an inverse link function does in a regression.\ngroup_by=nothing: a column of the table that the sessions were split from, or a tuple of columns, whose values group the sessions, so that each group has a group-level distribution of its own, such as :diagnosis for a clinical and a non-clinical group. Each session must have one value of each of the columns. Groups are numbered in the order of their first session. With nothing, every session is in one group.\ncentered=nothing: for the default normal distribution, whether the value of each session is sampled from the distribution of its group, with true, or as a standardized value which the group's mean shifts and its standard deviation scales, with false, which is the default, since it usually samples better when the data of a session informs its parameters little. Any other distribution is always sampled from directly, and giving centered with one is an error.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, with group_by as a tuple of column names, which is empty for one group, centered as true or false, and a distribution that is given as a type inside a function which makes it.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal, Exponential, Laplace\n\njulia> learning_rate = HierarchicalParameter((; mean = Normal(0, 1), sd = Exponential(1)); transformation = exp, group_by = :diagnosis);\n\njulia> learning_rate.group_by, learning_rate.centered\n((:diagnosis,), false)\n\njulia> inverse_temperature = HierarchicalParameter((; location = Normal(0, 1), scale = Exponential(1)); distribution = Laplace, transformation = exp);\n\njulia> inverse_temperature.centered\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.HierarchicalParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.HierarchicalRegression","page":"Fitting","text":"HierarchicalRegression(parameters)\n\nThe session parameter model of a full model which gives each parameter of every session by a regression on columns of the table that the sessions were split from, such as the age and the diagnosis of a participant, with random intercepts. parameters is a NamedTuple with a RegressionParameter for each parameter that is fitted, which has its intercept, its predictors, its random intercepts and their priors. It is kept in the field parameters. Useful for estimating the effect of a predictor on a parameter, or the difference between groups, together with the parameters of the sessions.\n\nA full model builds the design of every regression once, before it is made, from the table that MultipleSessionsInfo keeps, so sessions that were prepared one at a time can only have an intercept and a session deviation. The field designs is nothing until then, and afterwards has, for each parameter:\n\ncoefficient_priors and design_columns: the prior of each coefficient, and the values it is multiplied by, one for every session, under the name of the coefficient. A continuous predictor has one coefficient, named after its column, such as age. A categorical predictor has one for each level other than its reference level, named after the column and the symbol name of the level, such as diagnosis_clinical.\npredictor_centers: the mean that was subtracted from each continuous predictor, when they are centered.\nreference_levels: the reference level of each categorical predictor.\nrandom_intercept_levels: for the column of each random intercept, the level of every session, level_of_each_session, the number of levels, n_levels, the symbol name of each level, level_symbol_names, and groups, which is nothing without groups and otherwise has the group of every level, group_of_each_level, the number of groups, n_groups, and the symbol name of each group, group_symbol_names.\nsession_deviation_levels: the same for the deviations of the sessions, where every session is a level, or nothing without them.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Turing\nusing LogExpFunctions: logistic\n\nsession_parameter_model = HierarchicalRegression((;\n    learning_rate = RegressionParameter(;\n        intercept = Normal(0, 1),\n        predictors = (; age = Normal(0, 0.5)),\n        categorical_predictors = (; diagnosis = Normal(0, 1)),\n        random_intercepts = (; participant = Exponential(1)),\n        session_deviation = RandomIntercept(Exponential(1); group_by = :diagnosis),\n        transformation = logistic,\n    ),\n    inverse_temperature = RegressionParameter(;\n        intercept = Normal(0, 1), session_deviation = Exponential(1), transformation = exp\n    ),\n))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.HierarchicalRegression"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.RandomIntercept","page":"Fitting","text":"RandomIntercept(sd_prior; group_by=nothing, centered=false)\n\nA random intercept of a RegressionParameter: a deviation for each value of a column, such as each participant, from a normal distribution around zero whose standard deviation is sampled from sd_prior.\n\nArguments\n\nsd_prior: the prior of the standard deviation of the deviations, such as Exponential(1).\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ngroup_by=nothing: a column of the table that the sessions were split from, or a tuple of columns, whose values group the deviations, so that each group has a standard deviation of its own, such as :diagnosis for a clinical and a non-clinical group of participants. The sessions with one value of the random intercept's column must all be in one group. Groups are numbered in the order of their first session. With nothing, the deviations share one standard deviation.\ncentered=false: whether each deviation is sampled from the normal distribution directly, with true, or as a standardized value which the standard deviation scales, with false. The standardized form usually samples better when the data informs each deviation little.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, with group_by as a tuple of column names, which is empty without groups.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Exponential\n\njulia> by_participant = RandomIntercept(Exponential(1); group_by = :diagnosis);\n\njulia> by_participant.group_by, by_participant.centered\n((:diagnosis,), false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.RandomIntercept"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.RegressionParameter","page":"Fitting","text":"RegressionParameter(; intercept, session_deviation, predictors=(;),\n                    categorical_predictors=(;), random_intercepts=(;),\n                    <keyword arguments>)\n\nHow one parameter of a HierarchicalRegression is given to every session: by a regression on columns of the table that the sessions were split from. The value of a session is the sum of the intercept, the effect of each predictor, the random intercept of its value of each of some columns, and a deviation of its own, with transformation applied to the sum. Each session must have one value of every column that is used, which isn't missing.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nintercept: the prior of the intercept, such as Normal(0, 1), or nothing for a regression without one.\nsession_deviation: the prior of the standard deviation of the deviations of the sessions, such as Exponential(1), or a RandomIntercept with that prior and other settings. The deviations let sessions with the same value of every column differ. With nothing, such sessions have the same value of the parameter.\npredictors=(;): the continuous predictors, as a NamedTuple from the column to the prior of its coefficient, such as (; age = Normal(0, 0.5)). The values of the column must be numbers. The columns can also be given without priors, as :age or (:age, :iq), and then take coefficient_prior.\ncategorical_predictors=(;): the categorical predictors, in the same forms, such as (; diagnosis = Normal(0, 1)). Each value of the column other than its reference level has a coefficient with that prior, which is its difference from the reference level, such as the difference between a clinical and a non-clinical group.\nrandom_intercepts=(;): the columns with a random intercept for each of their values, as a NamedTuple from the column to the prior of the standard deviation of its random intercepts, such as (; participant = Exponential(1)) for participants with several sessions each, or to a RandomIntercept with that prior and other settings.\ntransformation=identity: a function applied to the sum for each session, such as exp for a parameter that must be positive or logistic for one between 0 and 1, as an inverse link function does in a regression.\ncoefficient_prior=nothing: the prior of the coefficient of every predictor that is given without one.\ncenter_predictors=false: whether the mean over the sessions is subtracted from each continuous predictor, so that the intercept is the value at the mean of each.\nreference_levels=(;): the reference level of categorical predictors, as a NamedTuple from the column to the level, such as (; diagnosis = \"control\"). Any other has the value of its first session as its reference level.\nsum_to_zero_coding=false: whether the coefficients of each categorical predictor are differences from the mean of its levels, with true, instead of differences from the reference level. The difference of the reference level is then minus the sum of the others.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, except coefficient_prior: predictors and categorical_predictors are NamedTuples with the prior of each column, and each entry of random_intercepts, and session_deviation, is a RandomIntercept.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Normal, Exponential\n\njulia> learning_rate = RegressionParameter(; intercept = Normal(0, 1), predictors = (; age = Normal(0, 0.5)), categorical_predictors = (; diagnosis = Normal(0, 1)), random_intercepts = (; participant = Exponential(1)), session_deviation = Exponential(1));\n\njulia> keys(learning_rate.predictors), keys(learning_rate.categorical_predictors)\n((:age,), (:diagnosis,))\n\njulia> learning_rate.random_intercepts.participant.centered\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.RegressionParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.CompositeSessionParameters","page":"Fitting","text":"CompositeSessionParameters(parts...; named_parts...)\n\nThe session parameter model of a full model which gives some of the parameters with each of its parts, which are session parameter models, such as a GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters for the learning rate, an IndependentSessionParameters for the inverse temperature and a FixedSessionParameters for a bias. What the parts give is merged. The variables of a part in parts are named as that part names them, and those of a part given by keyword in named_parts are named under its keyword, such as noise.learning_rate.session for a part given as noise. No two parts can give the same parameter: this is checked when it is made, for the parts whose CognitiveModeling.parameter_names are known, and when it runs, for the others. It samples when any part does. The parts are kept in the field parts, as a tuple, with those in named_parts last.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Turing\n\nsession_parameter_model = CompositeSessionParameters(\n    IndependentSessionParameters((; learning_rate = Beta(2, 2))),\n    FixedSessionParameters((; inverse_temperature = 2.0)),\n)\n\n#The variables of the part given as noise are named under noise, as noise.bias.session\nsession_parameter_model = CompositeSessionParameters(\n    IndependentSessionParameters((; learning_rate = Beta(2, 2)));\n    noise = IndependentSessionParameters((; bias = Normal(0, 1))),\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.CompositeSessionParameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.parameter_names","page":"Fitting","text":"parameter_names(parameters_model)\n\nReturn the names of the parameters that parameters_model gives every session, as a tuple of Symbols, or nothing when they aren't known before it runs. prepare_full_model checks them against the parameters of the cognitive model and of the environment model, and CompositeSessionParameters checks that no two of its parts give the same one.\n\nImplementation\n\nA session parameter model of one's own can add a method of this function. The default returns nothing, so that nothing is checked before a fit.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.parameter_names(FixedSessionParameters((; scale = 2.0, offset = 0.0)))\n(:scale, :offset)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.parameter_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.estimates_parameters","page":"Fitting","text":"estimates_parameters(parameters_model)\n\nReturn whether parameters_model, a session parameter model, estimates the parameters it gives from the data, instead of being given their values: false for NoParameters, FixedSessionParameters and FixedParametersBySession, whether any part does for a CompositeSessionParameters, and true for every other kind. When the environment's parameters are estimated, a full model includes the environment in every session, so that the observations in the data inform them, and it warns when the parameters of the two models can have the same variables.\n\nImplementation\n\nA session parameter model of one's own can add a method of this function. The default returns true, and a model which only gives values returns false.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.estimates_parameters(FixedSessionParameters((; scale = 2.0)))\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.estimates_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_parameters_model","page":"Fitting","text":"prepare_parameters_model(parameters_model, sessions_info)\n\nReturn parameters_model prepared for the sessions in sessions_info, with what doesn't change between the evaluations of an inference backend's model worked out once, before the model is made. A FixedParametersBySession has the values of every session looked up, a GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters has the group of every session found, a HierarchicalRegression has the design of every regression built, and a CompositeSessionParameters has each of its parts prepared. Used by prepare_full_model.\n\nImplementation\n\nA session parameter model of one's own can add a method of this function, which returns a session parameter model that the inference backends have methods for. The default returns parameters_model as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_parameters_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_full_model-Tuple{Any, MultipleSessionsInfo}","page":"Fitting","text":"prepare_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                   environment_parameters_model=NoParameters(),\n                   downstream_model=NoDownstreamModel(),\n                   fixed_cognitive_parameters=(;),\n                   fixed_environment_parameters=(;))\n\nPrepare the parts of a full model once, before an inference backend makes its model of them, and return them in a NamedTuple with cognitive_parameters_model, environment_parameters_model, downstream_model and sessions_info. Useful for an inference backend, whose full model calls it, so that what doesn't change between the evaluations of the model is worked out once.\n\nIt checks that the two session parameter models and the downstream model are of the right kind. The values in fixed_cognitive_parameters and fixed_environment_parameters, such as (; initial_value = 0.3), are given to every session for the parameters they name: each session parameter model is combined with them in a CompositeSessionParameters, so a parameter is either fixed or given by its session parameter model, and a fixed parameter must be one in the model's attributes. It checks the parameters that each session parameter model gives, when CognitiveModeling.parameter_names knows them, against those of the cognitive model and of the environment model: a parameter that isn't in the model's attributes is an error, and so is one in its attributes which is neither given nor has a default value, unless it is a parameter of an environment that no session runs. It prepares each session parameter model for the sessions with CognitiveModeling.prepare_parameters_model. When environment_parameters_model estimates parameters, every session with an environment includes it in the returned sessions_info.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_full_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.n_sessions","page":"Fitting","text":"n_sessions(sessions_info)\n\nReturn how many sessions a MultipleSessionsInfo has, which a session parameter model gives the parameters of. Used by the session parameter models of the inference backends, such as those of IndependentSessionParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.n_sessions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.session_index","page":"Fitting","text":"session_index(sessions_info, session_id)\n\nReturn the index i of the session with the identifier session_id in a MultipleSessionsInfo, which is how the models of several sessions number it: its parameters are element i of each vector a session parameter model gives, and its saved trajectories are element i of what the sessions return. The identifier is the value in the session column, or a NamedTuple of the values when there are several, as in sessions_info.session_ids. Used by users reading the results of a fit, not by the models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol","page":"Fitting","text":"session_id_to_symbol(session_id)\n\nReturn the symbol name of a session, a Symbol made from its identifier: the value of a single session column, as in S1, and the name and the value of each of several columns, joined by _ within a column and by __ between columns, as in id_S1__block_2. The chains of a fit name the entries of a session after it, such as S1.learning_rate, and FixedParametersBySession can find the values of a session by it. After using CognitiveModeling: session_id_to_symbol, the learning rate of a session is chain[@varname($(session_id_to_symbol(session_id)).learning_rate)].\n\nExamples\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol(\"S1\")\n:S1\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol((; id = \"S1\", block = 2))\n:id_S1__block_2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.session_column_values","page":"Fitting","text":"session_column_values(sessions_info, column)\n\nReturn the values of column in the table that the sessions in sessions_info were split from, as one vector for each session, of its rows in the table. It is an error when the sessions were given already prepared, without a table, and when the table has no such column. Useful for a session parameter model that reads a column, such as one to group the sessions by, or the covariate of a regression. Used by read_one_value_of_each_session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_column_values"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.predict_session_trajectories","page":"Fitting","text":"predict_session_trajectories([rng,] model, chain;\n                             cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(),\n                             environment_save_history=SaveHistory())\n\nSimulate the sessions of model, an inference backend's model of fitted sessions, again with the parameters of every sample in chain, and return the trajectories they saved for every sample. This can be used for prior and posterior predictive checks.\n\nThe sessions are prepared with prepare_for_prediction, so that every action is simulated, and every observation too when the sessions have an environment, and they save what the two save histories select. The model of those sessions then runs once for each sample, with the sample's parameters, and any start that a model samples, and with every variable within the timesteps drawn again, as data is in a predictive check: an action imputed in the fit is simulated like the others. The trajectories the sessions return are kept. The sessions have at most the number of timesteps of their data, and fewer when a stopping_criterion of a model is met, since no timestep of a predictive session has data. For a prior predictive check, give a chain sampled from the prior.\n\nEach inference backend has methods for its own models and chains, which need its package to be loaded. Which models they take, and the form of the chain they return, are described in the documentation of the inference backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.predict_session_trajectories"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"Overview","text":"What users work with, by topic:\n\nDefining models: the types that a cognitive model or an environment model is written with, and the functions it implements.\nSimulation: running models, alone or together.\nSaving histories: what a simulation or a fitted session saves of each model.\nFitting: the sessions to fit, how the parameters of several sessions are given, and what runs after them.\nFitting with Turing.jl: the Turing.jl models of the sessions, and reading the trajectories they saved from a chain.\n\nThe functions and types that are only used inside the package are under Internals.\n\nPages = [\"defining_models.md\", \"simulation.md\", \"saving_histories.md\", \"fitting.md\", \"fitting_with_turing.md\"]","title":"API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling","page":"Overview","text":"Computational modeling of cognition and behavior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling"},{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Automatic-differentiation-and-Reactant","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"Simulations can be differentiated with ForwardDiff, ReverseDiff, Enzyme and Mooncake, and compiled with Reactant. Every commit is tested against every form of each backend, on the cases in test/ad_and_reactant/test_cases.jl: simulations with an environment, with sampled actions and rewards, with timestep sizes and with gradients of the sizes themselves, without states, with a state changed in place, with a selection of what is saved, with maps of names between the model and the environment, with an environment which ends the simulation, with a reward that isn't given on every timestep, and on single observations, and agents simulated with an environment or on single observations, with and without a history, and with a state saved with deepcopy. Code in the package's extensions is differentiated in suites of its own, each with its own environment, beside the other tests of the extension. The one for Turing.jl, in test/extensions/turing/ad, differentiates the log density of Turing.jl models of a session, in its turing_cases.jl: with the agent or the environment starting, with imputed actions, with an environment filling in observations, with an environment which doesn't run because nothing in the data is missing, with observations imputed from an environment's distributions, with a reward that isn't given on every timestep, and with a model which samples within each timestep, and of the full model of several sessions, also with an environment parameter shared by the sessions, and with parameters from the distributions of groups and from regressions. That keeps the package's own code tested without Turing.jl, since the backends work without it and with other probabilistic programming languages. Every gradient is compared with finite differences. Zygote isn't supported, because the package uses for loops.\n\nBackend Forms tested\nForwardDiff default, chunk size 1\nReverseDiff default, compiled tape\nEnzyme forward, reverse, and both with runtime activity\nMooncake forward, reverse\nReactant compiled value, and compiled Enzyme reverse gradient, on the cases without a Xoshiro","title":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant"},{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Known-failures","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"These are bugs in the backends, which the package doesn't work around. Their tests are marked broken in runtests.jl of test/ad_and_reactant/ad and of test/extensions/turing/ad, so that the suites pass while the bug is there, and a fix shows up as an unexpected pass. Last checked with Julia 1.13.3, ReverseDiff 1.18.10, Enzyme 0.13.219, Mooncake 0.5.70, Turing 0.49.0 and DynamicPPL 0.42.14.\n\nBackend When What happens Status\nEnzyme reverse, with and without runtime activity simulate when the agent starts, actions and rewards are sampled from one seeded Xoshiro, and the number of timesteps is a constant wrong gradient, no error reported as Enzyme.jl issue 3912\nEnzyme forward with runtime activity simulate! of an agent with an environment, when the model's state starts as a constant such as (; expected_reward = 0.5) wrong gradient, no error reported as Enzyme.jl issue 3911\nEnzyme reverse with runtime activity the same throws a MethodError from create_shadow_ret reported as Enzyme.jl issue 3911\nEnzyme reverse, with and without runtime activity numbers copied into a vector whose element type is a Union: in simulate when an observation is NoValue on some timesteps and a number on the others, and the observations are saved, and when a vector of timestep sizes is returned after BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0, which it is by default throws an EnzymeNoTypeError reported as Enzyme.jl issue 4032; with a single timestep size, or save_initial_timestep_values = false, the sizes are right\nEnzyme forward and reverse without runtime activity the log density of a Turing.jl model of a session with an imputed action, an environment, or a model which samples within a timestep, and of the full model throws an EnzymeRuntimeActivityError a limitation Turing.jl documents: use runtime activity. A plain session passes\nEnzyme reverse, with and without runtime activity, and Enzyme forward when the agent starts a session whose observations are NoValue on some timesteps, so that a row of the data is one of two NamedTuples throws an IllegalTypeAnalysisException reported as Enzyme.jl issue 4031; an action that is MissingValue on some timesteps is fine\n\nA state which depends on the parameters from the start isn't affected, and neither is simulate! on single observations. A simulation where an observation isn't made on every timestep is right on every backend when the observations aren't saved, and on Enzyme's forward mode when they are. The other forms of Enzyme, and the other backends, are right on all of these cases. ForwardDiff, ReverseDiff and Mooncake are right on every Turing.jl case, and so are both forms of Enzyme with runtime activity on a session where every column of the data has one type.","title":"Known failures"},{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Pitfalls","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"These aren't failures of the package's own code, but a model can run into them.\n\nEnzyme reverse with runtime activity and a softmax in one line: a softmax written as exp.(v .- maximum(v)) ./ sum(exp.(v .- maximum(v))) gives wrong gradients, with no error, when it runs in a loop on values that differ between the timesteps. The softmax of LogExpFunctions.jl is right, and so is any softmax which keeps its exponentials in a variable and divides them by their sum. It is reported as Enzyme.jl issue 4030.\nCompiled ReverseDiff tapes: a tape records one path through the code, so a model with a branch on a value gives wrong gradients at points where the other branch is taken, with no error. The tests prepare the tape at a different point to catch this.\nTuring.jl models and Enzyme: Enzyme needs runtime activity to differentiate a Turing.jl model, as Turing's own documentation says; without it, it throws an EnzymeRuntimeActivityError.\nTuring.jl models and Mooncake: Mooncake can't differentiate logjoint(model, values) when the model has latent variables under an indexed prefix, such as the imputed actions of a session or a model which samples within a timestep, because building the variables runs DynamicPPL's warning about growable arrays, and a @warn holds a try. Samplers go through a LogDensityFunction, where the variables exist already, and that is fine. The same try is why turing_catch_invalid_parameters is only used when asked for, and a compiled ReverseDiff tape doesn't see the error it catches either, so the rejected parameters get a finite log density with no error.\nCatching invalid parameters: turing_catch_invalid_parameters, and a full model with catch_invalid_parameters = true, work with ForwardDiff and with Enzyme. Anything that uses what the model returns after a rejection, which is nothing, can't be differentiated with Enzyme, so return or stop at once when it is nothing. Mooncake can't differentiate a model with a try block at all, and a compiled ReverseDiff tape doesn't see the error, so the rejected parameters get a finite log density with no warning.\nTypes known when a session is compiled: a session costs about 10 ns per timestep to evaluate, with or without latent variables, as long as every type is known when it is compiled. Earlier versions of the package built a NamedTuple from names only known at run time, which made every timestep about a hundred times slower, and that cost was taken for one of DynamicPPL.jl's. The rows of the data get the element types of their columns for the same reason, so a column which holds two types costs nothing extra. A model's own code can lose the same factor in the same way, so check a slow model with JET's @report_opt.\nReactant: the constructors of Distributions.jl don't accept traced numbers, so a model built on them can't be compiled, and sets reactant = false in its test case. An if on a traced value throws, where ifelse works. A Xoshiro can't run in a compiled function, and the default generator repeats its numbers on every run, so pass a Reactant.ReactantRNG() as a positional argument. Compile times grow with the number of timesteps, because the loop is unrolled, so a few hundred timesteps is the practical limit.","title":"Pitfalls"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Models-from-ReinforcementLearning.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"An agent or a learner from ReinforcementLearning.jl can do the learning inside a cognitive model. The cognitive model keeps their object in its state, gives it what was observed on each timestep, and returns the probabilities of its actions, which a simulation samples from and fitting scores the recorded actions against.\n\nThis page shows two such cognitive models. It doesn't simulate or fit them yet, and its code isn't run when the documentation is built, because ReinforcementLearning.jl is a large package to have in the documentation's environment. The package's tests run it instead, with ReinforcementLearningCore.jl 0.15.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Categorical\nusing LogExpFunctions: softmax\nusing ReinforcementLearningBase: ReinforcementLearningBase as RLBase\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: ReinforcementLearningCore as RLCore\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: Agent, QBasedPolicy, TDLearner, TabularQApproximator\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: EpsilonGreedyExplorer, PreEpisodeStage, PostActStage\nusing ReinforcementLearningTrajectories: Trajectory, CircularArraySARTSTraces\nusing ReinforcementLearningTrajectories: DummySampler, InsertSampleRatioController\n\nThe attributes of both models have the observations observation, reward and terminated as inputs, which are the names that CommonRLEnvironment gives, so they can be used with an environment from ReinforcementLearning.jl as they are. The observation must be the number of the state the environment is in, since the learners here keep a table of values.\n\nBoth also need the environment to move first on each timestep, with EnvironmentStarts, so that there is an observation to act on. On the first timestep of an episode there is no reward yet: the reward is then NoValue, and the models check for AbstractNoValue, which covers that.","title":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#A-whole-agent","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"An agent from ReinforcementLearning.jl reads the state, the reward and the end of the episode from an environment object, at the stages of its own run loop. This small environment holds what the cognitive model observed on a timestep, for the agent to read.\n\nstruct ObservedByAgent <: RLBase.AbstractEnv\n    state::Int\n    reward::Float64\n    terminated::Bool\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nRLBase.state(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.state\nRLBase.reward(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.reward\nRLBase.is_terminated(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.terminated\nRLBase.action_space(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = Base.OneTo(rl_env.n_actions)\n\nThe cognitive model creates the agent in its initial state, from its parameters: here a temporal difference learner with a table of action values, which explores with a probability of epsilon.\n\nstruct TemporalDifferenceAgent <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    n_states::Int\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, parameters)\n\n    #The learner, with the learning rate and the discount factor from the parameters\n    rl_approximator = TabularQApproximator(;\n        n_state=model.n_states, n_action=model.n_actions\n    )\n    rl_learner = TDLearner(\n        rl_approximator, :SARS; α=parameters.learning_rate, γ=parameters.discount\n    )\n\n    #The explorer, which picks any action with a probability of epsilon\n    rl_explorer = EpsilonGreedyExplorer(parameters.epsilon; is_break_tie=true)\n\n    #The agent's memory of the last transition, which the learner learns from\n    rl_traces = CircularArraySARTSTraces(;\n        capacity=1,\n        state=Int64 => (),\n        action=Int64 => (),\n        reward=Float64 => (),\n        terminal=Bool => (),\n    )\n    rl_trajectory = Trajectory(rl_traces, DummySampler(), InsertSampleRatioController())\n\n    return (; rl_agent=Agent(QBasedPolicy(rl_learner, rl_explorer), rl_trajectory))\nend\n\nOn each timestep, the cognitive model takes the agent through the stage of its run loop that fits what was observed, and returns the probabilities its policy gives each action. The agent is changed in place, so the same object is passed on as the new state.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(\n    model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, context::ModelContext\n)\n\n    # Unpack the context ##\n    rl_agent = context.previous_state.rl_agent\n    (; observation, reward, terminated) = context.input\n\n    # Update state ##\n    if reward isa AbstractNoValue\n        #An episode starts, so there is nothing to learn from yet\n        rl_env = ObservedByAgent(observation, 0.0, false, model.n_actions)\n        push!(rl_agent, PreEpisodeStage(), rl_env)\n    else\n        #Give the agent what followed its previous action, and let it learn from it\n        rl_env = ObservedByAgent(observation, reward, terminated, model.n_actions)\n        push!(rl_agent, PostActStage(), rl_env, context.previous_output.move)\n        RLBase.optimise!(rl_agent, PostActStage())\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The policy gives a distribution over the actions, which the action is sampled from\n    Pₜ = RLBase.prob(rl_agent.policy, rl_env)\n\n    return ModelOutput((; move=Pₜ), (; rl_agent))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:learning_rate, :discount, :epsilon),\n        states=:rl_agent,\n        inputs=(:observation, :reward, :terminated),\n        outputs=:move,\n    )\nend\n\nSince the agent is changed in place, every saved state would be the same object, showing the agent after the last timestep. This method saves a copy of the agent on each timestep instead, made with deepcopy. It keeps the name rl_agent, since a SaveHistory selects states by the names in attribute_names. The table of action values of a saved agent is in rl_agent.policy.learner.approximator.model.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, state)\n    return (; rl_agent=deepcopy(state.rl_agent))\nend","title":"A whole agent"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Only-the-learner","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"A learner can also be used on its own, with one transition at a time, which needs no environment object and no trajectory. The action probabilities are then the cognitive model's own: here a softmax over the learner's action values, with an inverse temperature. The softmax is the one from LogExpFunctions.jl.\n\nstruct TemporalDifferenceLearner <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    n_states::Int\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, parameters)\n    rl_approximator = TabularQApproximator(;\n        n_state=model.n_states, n_action=model.n_actions\n    )\n    rl_learner = TDLearner(\n        rl_approximator, :SARS; α=parameters.learning_rate, γ=parameters.discount\n    )\n\n    #The learner needs the observation before the action too, so that is kept in the state\n    return (; rl_learner, previous_observation=0)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(\n    model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, context::ModelContext\n)\n\n    # Unpack the context ##\n    (; rl_learner, previous_observation) = context.previous_state\n    (; observation, reward, terminated) = context.input\n    β = context.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    # Update state ##\n    #Let the learner learn from what followed the previous action, unless an episode starts\n    if !(reward isa AbstractNoValue)\n        rl_transition = (;\n            state=previous_observation,\n            next_state=observation,\n            action=context.previous_output.move,\n            reward,\n            terminal=terminated,\n        )\n        RLBase.optimise!(rl_learner, rl_transition)\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The learner's value of each action where the agent is now\n    Qₜ = RLCore.forward(rl_learner, observation)\n    #Softmax over the action values\n    Pₜ = Categorical(softmax(β .* Qₜ))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; move=Pₜ), (; rl_learner, previous_observation=observation))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=(:learning_rate, :discount, :inverse_temperature),\n        states=(:rl_learner, :previous_observation),\n        inputs=(:observation, :reward, :terminated),\n        outputs=:move,\n    )\nend\n\nThe learner is changed in place too, so a copy of it is saved in the same way.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, state)\n    return (; rl_learner=deepcopy(state.rl_learner), state.previous_observation)\nend","title":"Only the learner"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Fitting","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"This page doesn't fit the cognitive models yet. When they are fitted with a sampler that uses gradients, use Enzyme.jl in reverse mode with runtime activity as the automatic differentiation backend, which for Turing.jl is AutoEnzyme(; mode = set_runtime_activity(Reverse), function_annotation = Const). It gave the right gradient of the log density of a session for both cognitive models here, when checked against finite differences with Enzyme.jl 0.13. ForwardDiff.jl and ReverseDiff.jl can't differentiate either of them, because the learner keeps its learning rate and its discount in Float64 fields, which the number types of those two don't fit in.\n\nUse the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl, as above, and not one written by hand. Written in one line, a softmax can get a wrong gradient from Enzyme's reverse mode, with no error, which the page Automatic differentiation and Reactant describes under its pitfalls.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/cognitive_abm_with_agents/#Cognitive-agent-based-modeling-with-Agents.jl","page":"Cognitive agent-based modeling with Agents.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to put a CognitiveAgent into an agent-based model from Agents.jl, with what each agent observes in the world and what its actions do to it.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Cognitive agent-based modeling with Agents.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#Internals-of-saving-histories","page":"Saving histories","text":"Pages = [\"saving_histories.md\"]","title":"Internals of saving histories"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#Selecting-what-is-saved","page":"Saving histories","text":"","title":"Selecting what is saved"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/saving_histories/#Storing-what-is-saved","page":"Saving histories","text":"","title":"Storing what is saved"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.as_selection-Tuple{SaveEverything}","page":"Saving histories","text":"as_selection(names)\n\nReturn names as a selection, which is how a SaveHistory keeps it: SaveEverything as it is, and otherwise a Tuple of Symbols, as as_tuple makes it. Used by the constructor of SaveHistory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_selection"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.check_savehistory_selection_names-Tuple{SaveHistory, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Saving histories","text":"check_savehistory_selection_names(save_history, model)\n\nThrow an informative error if save_history selects a state, an input, an output or an output distribution by name that isn't in the attributes of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, from attribute_names, since a history starts from those names, and prepare_state_for_saving returns a state with them. Used by InstantiatedModel, simulate and SessionInfo, before anything is saved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_savehistory_selection_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.check_selection_names-Tuple{SaveEverything, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Saving histories","text":"check_selection_names(selection, names, kind, model)\n\nThrow an informative error if selection has a name that isn't among names, those of kind in the attributes of model, where kind is \"states\", \"inputs\", \"outputs\" or \"output distributions\", the last being named like the outputs. SaveEverything can always be selected. Used by check_savehistory_selection_names for each kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_selection_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.each_timestep_size-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Saving histories","text":"each_timestep_size(timestep_sizes, n_steps)\n\nReturn the size of each of n_steps timesteps as a vector, for a history. A vector of sizes is returned as it is, and anything else is repeated for every timestep. Used by simulate!, for the sizes it adds to the histories, and by saved_timestep_sizes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.each_timestep_size"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.returned_timestep_sizes-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any, Val{false}}","page":"Saving histories","text":"returned_timestep_sizes(timestep_sizes, n_timesteps, save_initial_timestep_values::Val)\n\nReturn the sizes of the n_timesteps timesteps that ran, as a simulation returns them. A vector of sizes is cut to those timesteps, since a model can stop a simulation early, and starts with BeforeFirstTimestep for timestep 0 when save_initial_timestep_values is Val(true), so that it has an element for each element of what is saved. Anything else is returned as it is, since it is the size of every timestep. Used by simulate, simulate! and saved_timestep_sizes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.returned_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.save_state-Tuple{Any, Any, Val}","page":"Saving histories","text":"save_state(model, state, selection::Val)\n\nReturn what is saved of state: what selection selects of the output of prepare_state_for_saving. When nothing is selected, the state isn't prepared at all, so that no copy is made of a state which isn't saved. Used wherever a state is saved, by simulations, instantiated models and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.save_state"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.saved_timestep_sizes-Tuple{Any, Any, SaveHistory}","page":"Saving histories","text":"saved_timestep_sizes(timestep_sizes, n_steps, save_history)\n\nReturn the sizes of n_steps timesteps as they are returned with a history: one size for each timestep when save_history saves anything, after BeforeFirstTimestep for timestep 0 when it saves the initial timestep values, and an empty vector when it saves nothing. Used by SessionInfo, for the sizes a fitted session returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saved_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.saves_initial_timestep_values-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_timestep_values}}, Tuple{Tsave_initial_timestep_values}, Tuple{Toutput_distributions}, Tuple{Toutputs}, Tuple{Tinputs}, Tuple{Tstates}} where {Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_timestep_values}","page":"Saving histories","text":"saves_initial_timestep_values(save_history)\n\nReturn whether save_history saves the values from before the first timestep, as timestep 0. Used by simulate, InstantiatedModel, saved_timestep_sizes and fitted sessions, to decide whether timestep 0 is saved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saves_initial_timestep_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.saves_nothing-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{(), (), (), (), Tsave_initial_timestep_values}}, Tuple{Tsave_initial_timestep_values}} where Tsave_initial_timestep_values","page":"Saving histories","text":"saves_nothing(save_history)\n\nReturn whether save_history selects nothing of any kind. Whether it saves the initial timestep values doesn't matter, since there is nothing to save of them. Used by simulate!, InstantiatedModel and fitted sessions, which save nothing at all then.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saves_nothing"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.select_names-Tuple{Any, Val{SaveEverything()}}","page":"Saving histories","text":"select_names(x, selection::Val)\n\nReturn what a SaveHistory selects of x: x itself for SaveEverything, an empty NamedTuple for no names, and the named entries of a NamedTuple for a tuple of names. Used wherever something is saved, by simulations, instantiated models and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.state_selection-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_timestep_values}}, Tuple{Tsave_initial_timestep_values}, Tuple{Toutput_distributions}, Tuple{Toutputs}, Tuple{Tinputs}, Tuple{Tstates}} where {Tstates, Tinputs, Toutputs, Toutput_distributions, Tsave_initial_timestep_values}","page":"Saving histories","text":"state_selection(save_history)\ninput_selection(save_history)\noutput_selection(save_history)\noutput_distribution_selection(save_history)\n\nReturn the selection of one kind in a SaveHistory as a Val. Used wherever something is saved, by simulations, instantiated models and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.state_selection"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_storage-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Saving histories","text":"initialize_storage(first_value, n_steps)\ninitialize_storage(initial_value, first_value, n_steps)\n\nCreate the vector for saving one kind of value over n_steps timesteps, using the value of the first timestep to set its type. With initial_value, what is saved from before the first timestep, the vector starts with it, as timestep 0, and its type holds both. Used by simulations and by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.push_value!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Saving histories","text":"push_value!(storage, value)\n\nAdd value at the end of storage, and return the vector. As for store_value!, a value that doesn't fit the type of storage moves the values so far to a wider vector, which is returned instead. Starting from Vector{Union{}}() lets the first value set the type. Used by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.push_value!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.store_value!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"Saving histories","text":"store_value!(storage, value, t)\n\nSave value as element t of storage, and return the vector.\n\nIf the value doesn't fit the type of storage, the values so far are moved to a wider vector which can hold both types, and that vector is returned instead. Used by simulations and by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_value!"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Planned-tutorials-and-interfaces","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"This page lists what is planned and not written yet. The list will grow.","title":"Planned tutorials and interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Cognitive-models-for-tutorials","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"Models of metacognition, such as HMeta-d.\nThe models of hBayesDM, probably every one of them.\nThe main models for the Iowa Gambling Task, such as PVL-Delta and ORL.\nDifferent variants of the Rescorla-Wagner model, and models like it.\nEvidence accumulation models, such as attention-based models.\nBayesian models made with RxInfer.jl.\nActive inference models, which a separate package is being made for.\nAn example of a cognitive model which uses Turing.jl to select its actions.\nModels with discrete parameters, to show how they are fitted.","title":"Cognitive models for tutorials"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Interfaces","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"BayesianRegressionModels.jl.\nSimulation-based inference, where a neural network is trained on simulations to estimate the parameters: NeuralEstimators.jl, to test or to make an interface with, and perhaps the Python package sbi too. A simulation of a cognitive model is what both need.\nStanBlocks.jl, potentially, as another way to write the probabilistic model around a session.\nGlobalSensitivity.jl, for Sobol sensitivity analyses of cognitive models: how much each parameter, alone and together with the others, changes what a model does.\nCognitive models in continuous time, probably with DifferentialEquations.jl, whose states change between the timesteps as well as on them.","title":"Interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Defining-models","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"A macro that defines a cognitive model or an environment in one command, and writes the methods of the functions to extend where it can deduce them, such as attribute_names from the names the model uses.","title":"Defining models"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Fitting","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"Dyadic interaction, with another agent as the environment of the agent that is fitted. When the other agent's actions are recorded, each agent is a session of its own whose observations are the other's actions. When they aren't, the other agent is the environment that fills them in.\nSession identifiers in the names of the variables of a fitted session, instead of session[i], if a way is found that doesn't compile the session once per identifier.\nA convenient way to fit a single session, with the full model or a variant of it.\nChoosing a sampler for each parameter from whether it is discrete or continuous.\nMixture models and model selection within the fitted model, in the style of Michael D. Lee: a latent variable for each session, or each group, chooses which of several cognitive models generated its behavior, and its posterior compares the models.\nSeveral sets of experimental data in one hierarchical model, such as different experiments or tasks done by the same participants, which share the parameters they have in common.\nFormulas for a HierarchicalRegression, as an extension for StatsModels.jl, so that the regression of a parameter can be written as @formula(learning_rate ~ 1 + age + diagnosis + (1 | participant)).","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Plots","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"A plot of the parameters of every fitted session, with the averages of their groups.\nA plot of the trajectories of different fitted sessions over the timesteps.\nPlots of a simulation, a history or a fitted session over time. The entry before the first timestep is timestep 0, at time 0, and its size is BeforeFirstTimestep(). The time of a later timestep is the sum of the sizes of the timesteps up to it.","title":"Plots"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/compiling_with_reactant/#Compiling-simulations-with-Reactant.jl","page":"Compiling simulations with Reactant.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to compile a simulation with Reactant.jl, and how to take its gradient with Enzyme.jl. Until then, this is what to know before trying.\n\nShort simulations can be compiled with Reactant.jl, if the action distributions work with traced numbers, which those from Distributions.jl don't. Compile a function that takes a Reactant.ReactantRNG() as a positional argument and passes it on as rng, because @jit doesn't trace keyword arguments, and the default generator repeats its numbers on every run. Compile times grow quickly with n_steps, so this is for a few hundred timesteps at most.\n\nThe page Automatic differentiation and Reactant lists what is tested, and the pitfalls a model can run into.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Compiling simulations with Reactant.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Defining-models","page":"Defining models","text":"These are what a cognitive model or an environment model is written with, since the two are defined in the same way.\n\nPages = [\"defining_models.md\"]","title":"Defining models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Types","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Functions-that-a-model-must-implement","page":"Defining models","text":"These and the functions that a model can implement are public, but not exported, so use them with the package's name, as in CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model.","title":"Functions that a model must implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Functions-that-a-model-can-implement","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Functions that a model can implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Reading-a-model's-parameters","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Reading a model's parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"api/defining_models/#Environments-from-other-packages","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Environments from other packages"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractCognitiveModel","page":"Defining models","text":"AbstractCognitiveModel\n\nSupertype for cognitive models, whose inputs are observations and whose outputs are actions.\n\nIn the REPL, a cognitive model shows its type, the values of its fields if it has any, and the names of its attributes, or not specified if it has no method of attribute_names. A kind of attribute with more than five names shows only how many there are, and attribute_names gives them. On one line, as inside a vector, a model shows its type and its kind.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype and add methods of apply_timestep_model, initialize_state and attribute_names for it. A model can also add methods of prepare_state_for_saving, default_parameters and stopping_criterion. Fitting with a PPL can need methods of its own, which the documentation for that PPL describes.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct ExampleModel <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::ExampleModel)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = (:learning_rate, :noise),\n               states = :expected_reward,\n               inputs = :reward,\n               outputs = (),\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> ExampleModel()\n-- Cognitive model --\nType: ExampleModel\nParameters: learning_rate, noise\nStates: expected_reward\nInputs: reward\nOutputs: none\n\njulia> print(ExampleModel())\nExampleModel (cognitive model)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractCognitiveModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractEnvironmentModel","page":"Defining models","text":"AbstractEnvironmentModel\n\nSupertype for environment models, whose inputs are the actions of a cognitive model and whose outputs are its observations.\n\nAn environment model shows in the REPL and on one line as a cognitive model does, with its own kind.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype and add the same methods as for an AbstractCognitiveModel: apply_timestep_model, initialize_state and attribute_names, and optionally prepare_state_for_saving, default_parameters and stopping_criterion. Fitting with a PPL can need methods of its own, which the documentation for that PPL describes.\n\nSee also simulate, which has an example.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct ExampleEnvironment <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::ExampleEnvironment)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = :reward_probability,\n               states = (),\n               inputs = :choice,\n               outputs = :reward,\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> ExampleEnvironment()\n-- Environment model --\nType: ExampleEnvironment\nParameters: reward_probability\nStates: none\nInputs: choice\nOutputs: reward\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractEnvironmentModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractNoValue","page":"Defining models","text":"AbstractNoValue\n\nSupertype for what stands in for an observation or an action that isn't there: NoValue and BeforeFirstTimestep, and in the data of a fitted session UnimputedMissingValue. A model which treats them all the same can check for it, as in context.input.reward isa AbstractNoValue.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractNoValue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AgentStarts","page":"Defining models","text":"AgentStarts()\n\nMake the agent move first on each timestep of a simulation or a fitted session.\n\nOn a timestep, the cognitive model updates and acts on the previous observation, and the environment then responds to its action. On the first timestep, the cognitive model receives an observation with every entry BeforeFirstTimestep. Both models receive it as the who_starts of their ModelContext, so that a cognitive model can act before it updates when that is what it means.\n\nSee also EnvironmentStarts, UnknownWhoStarts, simulate and SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AgentStarts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.BeforeFirstTimestep","page":"Defining models","text":"BeforeFirstTimestep()\n\nStand-in for an observation or an action from before the first timestep, when there is none yet, as one entry of a NamedTuple, such as (; choice = BeforeFirstTimestep()).\n\nA model receives it for every previous output on its first timestep, and for every input too when the other side hasn't moved yet: a cognitive model when the agent starts, and an environment model when the environment starts. NoValue is different: it says that nothing was observed or done on a timestep that did run.\n\nBefore the first timestep is timestep 0, which a simulation, a history and a fitted session start with when their SaveHistory saves the initial timestep values, as it does by default. Its observation and action are this marker unless others are given, and so is its size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.BeforeFirstTimestep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.Deterministic","page":"Defining models","text":"Deterministic(value)\n\nAn action or an observation that is always value, for a cognitive model or an environment model which gives it without any randomness, as in (; reward = Deterministic(1.0)).\n\nA simulation samples every action and every observation with rand, and sampling from a Deterministic gives value itself. The value is kept in the field value.\n\nA simulation throws an error for a number, a string or a symbol that isn't wrapped, since none of them is something to sample from. A vector, a tuple or a range that isn't wrapped is sampled from as it is: rand picks one of its elements, with no error.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Random: Xoshiro\n\njulia> rand(Xoshiro(1), Deterministic(1.0))\n1.0\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Deterministic(true))).output_distribution.choice.value\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.Deterministic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentStarts","page":"Defining models","text":"EnvironmentStarts()\n\nMake the environment move first on each timestep of a simulation or a fitted session.\n\nOn a timestep, the environment gives an observation in response to the previous action, and the cognitive model then updates and acts on it. On the first timestep, the environment receives an action with every entry BeforeFirstTimestep. Both models receive it as the who_starts of their ModelContext.\n\nSee also AgentStarts, UnknownWhoStarts, simulate and SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentStarts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.InvalidParametersError","page":"Defining models","text":"InvalidParametersError(message)\nInvalidParametersError()\n\nAn error for a model to throw from apply_timestep_model or initialize_state when its parameters aren't valid, as in throw(InvalidParametersError()).\n\nThe default message says that the parameters are not valid, possibly dependent on the observations, actions and states, and a model can give a more specific one. A simulation stops with it as with any other error, and fitting can catch it to give the parameters a probability of zero, so a model should throw this rather than an ordinary error when the parameters are the problem.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> try\n           throw(InvalidParametersError())\n       catch error\n           sprint(showerror, error)\n       end\n\"InvalidParametersError: Model parameters are not valid, possibly dependent on observations, actions and states.\"\n\njulia> sprint(showerror, InvalidParametersError(\"The learning rate must be between 0 and 1.\"))\n\"InvalidParametersError: The learning rate must be between 0 and 1.\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.InvalidParametersError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ModelAttributes","page":"Defining models","text":"ModelAttributes(; parameters, states, inputs, outputs)\n\nThe names of the attributes of a model, which attribute_names returns.\n\nFor a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it receives and the outputs the observations it gives.\n\nEach argument is a Tuple of Symbols, read as a property of the same name, such as attributes.parameters. A single Symbol is put in a tuple, and a Vector of them is made into one. All four must be given, and () means that the model has none of that kind. A name can only be used once across the four kinds, so that it says which attribute of the model is meant wherever the attributes are named together, and an ArgumentError is thrown otherwise.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> attributes = ModelAttributes(;\n           parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature),\n           states = :expected_reward,\n           inputs = [:reward],\n           outputs = (),\n       )\nModelAttributes\nParameters: learning_rate, inverse_temperature\nStates: expected_reward\nInputs: reward\nOutputs: none\n\njulia> attributes.states\n(:expected_reward,)\n\njulia> ModelAttributes(; parameters = :choice, states = (), inputs = (), outputs = :choice)\nERROR: ArgumentError: The name choice is used for both a parameter and an output, but a model can only use a name for one attribute.\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelAttributes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ModelContext","page":"Defining models","text":"ModelContext(; input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters, timestep_size=1,\n             rng=default_rng(), who_starts=UnknownWhoStarts())\nModelContext(input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters, timestep_size=1,\n             rng=default_rng(), who_starts=UnknownWhoStarts())\n\nEverything a model needs to run a single timestep, which apply_timestep_model receives. The arguments can be given by name, which says what each one is, or in this order.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as context.input. For a cognitive model the input is its observation and the output its action, and for an environment model the input is the action of the cognitive model and the output the observation it gives.\n\nArguments\n\ninput: what the model receives on this timestep, in a NamedTuple with the names the other side gave the entries, as in context.input.reward. An entry that isn't there on this timestep is NoValue. On the first timestep, when the other side hasn't moved yet, every entry is BeforeFirstTimestep.\nprevious_state: the state of the model after the previous timestep. On the first timestep, this is the output of initialize_state.\nprevious_output: what the model gave on the previous timestep, in a NamedTuple with the names the model gave the entries, as in context.previous_output.choice. On the first timestep, each of them is BeforeFirstTimestep.\nparameters: the parameters of the model, in a NamedTuple.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers; the difference between the end of the previous and the end of the current timestep.\nrng=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from if anything in the model is random, so that a simulation can be reproduced.\nwho_starts=UnknownWhoStarts(): who moves first on the timestep, AgentStarts or EnvironmentStarts as the simulation or the fitted session was told, or UnknownWhoStarts when neither is known. A model which can update before it acts or act before it updates can choose by it: for a cognitive model, with EnvironmentStarts() the input is this timestep's observation, and with AgentStarts() the previous timestep's.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> input = (; reward = 1.0);\n\njulia> previous_state = (; expected_reward = 0.5);\n\njulia> previous_output = (; choice = BeforeFirstTimestep());\n\njulia> parameters = (; learning_rate = 0.1);\n\njulia> ModelContext(; input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters).timestep_size\n1\n\njulia> ModelContext(; input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters, timestep_size = 0.5).timestep_size\n0.5\n\njulia> ModelContext(input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters).who_starts\nUnknownWhoStarts()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelContext"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.ModelOutput","page":"Defining models","text":"ModelOutput(output_distribution, new_state=(;))\n\nThe outputs of a model for a single timestep, which apply_timestep_model returns.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as output.new_state.\n\nArguments\n\noutput_distribution::NamedTuple: what the model's outputs on this timestep are sampled from, in a NamedTuple with one entry for each output, such as (; choice = Bernoulli(0.7)) for the action of a cognitive model, or (; reward = Bernoulli(0.8)) for the observation an environment model gives. Each entry can be anything that works with rand, such as a distribution from Distributions.jl, or a Deterministic for an output that isn't random, and the sampled outputs keep the names. An output that isn't given on this timestep is NoValue, and a model without outputs gives an empty NamedTuple, (;).\nnew_state::NamedTuple=(;): the state of the model after this timestep, which is the previous_state of the next one, with one entry for each state in the model's attributes, also when the state is a single object, as in (; rl_env). A model without a state gives an empty NamedTuple, (;). The outputs are sampled after the state is made, so it can't depend on them: the model receives them as context.previous_output on the next timestep.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Bernoulli\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.7)), (; expected_reward = 0.5)).new_state\n(expected_reward = 0.5,)\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.7))).new_state\nNamedTuple()\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = NoValue(), bet = Bernoulli(0.2))).output_distribution.choice\nNoValue()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelOutput"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.NoValue","page":"Defining models","text":"NoValue()\n\nStand-in for an observation that isn't made, or an action that isn't taken, on a timestep, as one entry of the observations or the actions NamedTuple, such as (; reward = NoValue()) or (; choice = NoValue()). Whether it is an observation or an action is given by where it is. Before the first timestep, an observation or an action is BeforeFirstTimestep instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoValue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.UnknownWhoStarts","page":"Defining models","text":"UnknownWhoStarts()\n\nThe who_starts of a ModelContext when neither side is known to move first on a timestep, as when a model runs on a single input with simulate, or is used outside a simulation. AgentStarts and EnvironmentStarts are the other two, which a simulation or a fitted session passes on from its own who_starts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.UnknownWhoStarts"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model","page":"Defining models","text":"apply_timestep_model(model::AbstractTimestepModel, context::ModelContext)::ModelOutput\n\nRun a single timestep of model: update its state, and return what its outputs are sampled from. For a cognitive model the outputs are its actions, and for an environment model the observations it gives.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. A simulation samples the outputs itself, from the output_distribution of the ModelOutput that the method returns, so an output that isn't random is wrapped in a Deterministic. In simulations, anything else that is random, such as a change of the state, should be sampled from context.rng. If states are mutated in place, a prepare_state_for_saving method must be added. For use within PPLs, this function can also have special implementations, and there may be special constraints: see the documentation for the specific PPL for details.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: BernoulliLogit\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n\n    ## Unpack the context ##\n    #Reward observed on this timestep\n    r = context.input.reward\n    #Expected reward from the previous state\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    #Learning rate parameter\n    α = context.parameters.learning_rate\n    #Inverse temperature parameter\n    β = context.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    ## Update state ##\n    #Update expected reward using the Rescorla-Wagner rule\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + α * (r - V₋₁)\n\n    ## Compute action probabilities ##\n    #Softmax function, instantiated as a Bernoulli logit distribution\n    Pₜ = BernoulliLogit(β * (Vₜ - 0.5))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Pₜ), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nparameters = (; learning_rate = 0.1, inverse_temperature = 2.0)\ncontext = ModelContext(; input = (; reward = 1.0), previous_state = (; expected_reward = 0.5), previous_output = (; choice = NoValue()), parameters)\n\nCognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(RescorlaWagner(), context).new_state\n\n# output\n\n(expected_reward = 0.55,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_state","page":"Defining models","text":"initialize_state(model::AbstractTimestepModel, parameters)\n\nCreate the initial state that model has before its first timestep, which can depend on parameters.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. It returns a NamedTuple with one entry for each state in the model's attributes, also when the state is a single object, as in (; rl_env), and a model without a state returns an empty NamedTuple, (;). For use within PPLs, this function can also have special implementations: see the documentation for the specific PPL for details.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n           return (; expected_reward = parameters.initial_value)\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(RescorlaWagner(), (; initial_value = 0.5))\n(expected_reward = 0.5,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.attribute_names","page":"Defining models","text":"attribute_names(model::AbstractTimestepModel)::ModelAttributes\n\nReturn the names of the parameters, states, inputs and outputs of model, in a ModelAttributes.\n\nThe names define the model, so they must match what the model uses. The parameters are the names that apply_timestep_model and initialize_state read from the parameters. The states are the names in the state that initialize_state returns and in new_state, which apply_timestep_model reads from context.previous_state. The inputs are the names that apply_timestep_model reads from context.input, and the outputs are the names in output_distribution, which are also those in context.previous_output. For a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it receives and the outputs the observations it gives. Nothing checks that they match while the model runs.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. A model without one kind of attribute gives () for it. simulate and InstantiatedModel use the output names for the output before the first timestep, and check the parameters, the previous output and the state against the names. A simulation with an environment also checks that the two models fit together: the outputs of each must be the inputs of the other, unless a NameMap says which of them are passed on, and under which names.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature),\n               states = :expected_reward,\n               inputs = :reward,\n               outputs = :choice,\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(RescorlaWagner()).parameters\n(:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.attribute_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving","page":"Defining models","text":"prepare_state_for_saving(model::AbstractTimestepModel, state)\n\nReturn what a simulation saves of state, the state of a cognitive model or of an environment model, as it is about to save it. The state that the next timestep starts from isn't affected.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default returns state as it is, so nothing is copied. One which changes an object in its state in place must add a method which copies it, or all the saved states show the final values, with no error. The method must return the state with the names in the model's attributes, since a SaveHistory selects states by those names. Nothing checks this while the model runs.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct LearnsInPlace <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::LearnsInPlace, state)\n           return (; values = copy(state.values))\n       end;\n\njulia> state = (; values = [0.5, 0.5]);\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(LearnsInPlace(), state).values === state.values\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.default_parameters","page":"Defining models","text":"default_parameters(model::AbstractTimestepModel)\n\nReturn the default parameter values of a cognitive model or of an environment model, such as (; learning_rate = 0.1), which InstantiatedModel and simulate use for the parameters they aren't given.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default is an empty NamedTuple, (;), so there are no default values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.default_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.parameter_domains","page":"Defining models","text":"parameter_domains(model::AbstractTimestepModel)\n\nReturn the values that the parameters of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, can take: a NamedTuple from the name of a parameter to its domain, which is a NamedTuple with any of lower, the smallest value it can have, upper, the largest, and discrete, whether it must be a whole number. What a domain leaves out is unbounded and continuous, and so is a parameter without a domain.\n\nThe parameters of a simulation and of a fitted session are checked against their domains before anything runs, and a value outside its domain throws an InvalidParametersError. An inference backend can also warn before a fit when a prior gives values outside a domain, and CognitiveModeling.discrete_parameter_names gives the discrete parameters, which are sampled in another way than the continuous ones.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default is an empty NamedTuple, (;), so that every parameter can take any value. A domain is best kept to the values the model can't run without, since a value outside it can't be fitted at all.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct CountsOptions <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.parameter_domains(model::CountsOptions)\n           return (;\n               learning_rate = (; lower = 0, upper = 1),\n               n_options = (; lower = 1, discrete = true),\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.discrete_parameter_names(CountsOptions())\n(:n_options,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.parameter_domains"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion","page":"Defining models","text":"stopping_criterion(model::AbstractTimestepModel, state, parameters)\n\nReturn whether model, a cognitive model or an environment model, with state as its state after a timestep and its parameters, has signaled for a simulation to stop there instead of running all of n_steps.\n\nsimulate and simulate! check it for both models before each timestep, with the states that the timestep before left, unless they are given check_stopping_criterion = false. When it returns true for either, no further timestep runs, and what is returned and added to the histories has the timesteps that ran. When the environment starts, the cognitive model has then made its last observation and acted on it, and the environment doesn't receive that action. When the cognitive model starts, the environment has then responded to the last action with an observation, and the cognitive model doesn't receive that observation. A model whose criterion is already met before the first timestep can't be simulated, and throws an error: a new simulation starts from the initial state, and reset! puts an InstantiatedModel back there. A fitted session checks it as SessionInfo describes.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default returns false, so a simulation runs all of its timesteps.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Maze <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion(environment::Maze, state, parameters)\n           return state.position == parameters.goal\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion(Maze(), (; position = 4), (; goal = 4))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion"},{"category":"function","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.discrete_parameter_names","page":"Defining models","text":"discrete_parameter_names(model)\n\nReturn the names of the parameters of model that are discrete in parameter_domains, as a tuple. Every other parameter is continuous. Useful for choosing how each parameter is sampled, since a discrete parameter needs another kind of sampler than a continuous one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.discrete_parameter_names"},{"category":"type","location":"api/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.CommonRLEnvironment","page":"Defining models","text":"CommonRLEnvironment(rl_env::CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv; action_name)\n\nAn environment model around rl_env, an environment from CommonRLInterface.jl, which needs CommonRLInterface.jl to be loaded. action_name is the name of the cognitive model's action that act! receives, such as :arm.\n\nEnvironments from POMDPs.jl and ReinforcementLearning.jl can be used through it. POMDPTools.jl wraps a POMDP as POMDPCommonRLEnv{Any}(pomdp) and an MDP as MDPCommonRLEnv{Any}(mdp), which give each observation, or each state of an MDP, as the problem gives it. Its convert(CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv, pomdp) gives the same environment with each converted to an array, which a cognitive model that reads them as the problem gives them, such as one with a belief updater of POMDPTools.jl, can't use. ReinforcementLearningBase.jl defines convert(CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv, rl_env) for its environments.\n\nThe state is (; rl_env): initialize_state resets rl_env with CommonRLInterface.jl's reset! and returns it in the state, every timestep changes it in place with act! and passes it on in the new state, and prepare_state_for_saving copies it, with clone if it provides it and with deepcopy otherwise. Every simulation with the same CommonRLEnvironment therefore resets and changes the same rl_env, so simulations that run at the same time, such as on several threads, need one each. A method of prepare_state_for_saving for a CommonRLEnvironment with a specific type of rl_env can save something lighter. The parameters aren't used, and neither is the random number generator, since an environment of CommonRLInterface.jl has its own randomness.\n\nEach observation is a NamedTuple (; observation, reward, terminated): what observe gives, copied unless it is plain data such as a number, the reward that act! returned, and whether the episode has ended. Each is given in a Deterministic, so nothing more is sampled. Before the first action, when the action is BeforeFirstTimestep, the environment is at its initial observation, so the reward is NoValue and nothing is done with the action. The same goes for a timestep where the model takes no action, NoValue: the environment stays where it is. Its attribute_names are these three observations, the one action, no parameters and the state rl_env. Use EnvironmentStarts, so that the model observes before it acts.\n\nA simulation stops by itself when the episode ends, since the stopping_criterion of the environment is terminated of rl_env, so n_steps is the most timesteps it can take. An episode that has ended isn't started again, and an action given to it throws an error. A new call of simulate starts a new episode, and so does reset! of an InstantiatedModel, since both reset rl_env through initialize_state.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing CommonRLInterface\n\n#A two-armed bandit where the first arm always pays, in episodes of two pulls\nmutable struct Bandit <: CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv\n    pulls::Int\nend\nCommonRLInterface.reset!(rl_env::Bandit) = (rl_env.pulls = 0; nothing)\nCommonRLInterface.actions(rl_env::Bandit) = 1:2\nCommonRLInterface.observe(rl_env::Bandit) = rl_env.pulls\nCommonRLInterface.act!(rl_env::Bandit, arm) = (rl_env.pulls += 1; arm == 1 ? 1.0 : 0.0)\nCommonRLInterface.terminated(rl_env::Bandit) = rl_env.pulls == 2\n\n#A model which always pulls the first arm\nstruct PullsFirstArm <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\nCognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::PullsFirstArm, parameters) = (;)\nCognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(model::PullsFirstArm, context::ModelContext) = ModelOutput((; arm = Deterministic(1)))\nCognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::PullsFirstArm) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = (), inputs = (:observation, :reward, :terminated), outputs = :arm)\n\n#The simulation stops when the episode ends, before it has run all of n_steps\nsimulation = simulate(\n    PullsFirstArm(),\n    CommonRLEnvironment(Bandit(0); action_name = :arm);\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps = 10,\n    cognitive_parameters = (;),\n    environment_parameters = (;),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()),\n)\n\n#Timestep 0, before the first timestep, and then what the environment gave on each timestep\nforeach(println, simulation.observations)\n\n# output\n\n(observation = BeforeFirstTimestep(), reward = BeforeFirstTimestep(), terminated = BeforeFirstTimestep())\n(observation = 0, reward = NoValue(), terminated = false)\n(observation = 1, reward = 1.0, terminated = false)\n(observation = 2, reward = 1.0, terminated = true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.CommonRLEnvironment"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_actr_models/#Models-from-ACTRModels.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from ACTRModels.jl as cognitive models","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use ACTRModels.jl, a package for building ACT-R models, inside a cognitive model.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Models from ACTRModels.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/simulation/#Internals-of-simulation","page":"Simulation","text":"Pages = [\"simulation.md\"]","title":"Internals of simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/simulation/#Simulating","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"Simulating"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/simulation/#Instantiated-models-and-name-maps","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"Instantiated models and name maps"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.check_names_fit","page":"Simulation","text":"check_names_fit(cognitive_model, environment, observation_map=nothing,\n                action_map=nothing)\n\nThrow an informative error if cognitive_model and environment can't be simulated together. Without a map, the outputs of each must be the inputs of the other in attribute_names: the observations the environment gives are the inputs in the cognitive model's attributes, and the actions the cognitive model gives are the inputs in the environment's attributes. With a NameMap for the observations or the actions, the map must take names in the giver's attributes, and give exactly the names in the receiver's attributes. Used by simulate, simulate!, and SessionInfo for a session with an environment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_names_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.check_observation_names_fit-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Nothing}","page":"Simulation","text":"check_observation_names_fit(cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_model_names,\n                            environment_names, observation_map)\ncheck_action_names_fit(cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_model_names,\n                       environment_names, action_map)\n\nThrow an informative error if the observations environment gives, its outputs, don't fit the inputs in the attributes of cognitive_model, or the actions cognitive_model gives, its outputs, don't fit the inputs in the attributes of environment, as check_names_fit describes, with or without a NameMap. Used by check_names_fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_observation_names_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.check_stopping_criterion_not_met-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"check_stopping_criterion_not_met(model, state, parameters)\n\nThrow an informative error if stopping_criterion of model is already met with state, before the first timestep, since no timestep can then run. Used for each model by simulate_all_timesteps, and by fitted sessions, where there is nothing to check for an environment a session doesn't have.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_stopping_criterion_not_met"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.collect_initial_timestep_values-Tuple{Val{false}, Vararg{Any, 7}}","page":"Simulation","text":"collect_initial_timestep_values(save_initial_timestep_values::Val,\n                                previous_timestep_values, cognitive_model,\n                                environment, observation_map, action_map,\n                                cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nReturn what is saved from before the first timestep, as timestep 0, arranged as the saved values of collect_timestep_values, or nothing when save_initial_timestep_values is Val(false).\n\nThe observation and the action are the previous ones, as each side receives them, the output distributions are BeforeFirstTimestep for every output, since none was given yet, and the states are the states before the first timestep. They must be saved before the first timestep is simulated, since it can change a state in place. Used by simulate_all_timesteps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_initial_timestep_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.collect_timestep_values-NTuple{10, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"collect_timestep_values(environment_output, cognitive_output, environment_observation,\n                        observation, action, environment_action, cognitive_model,\n                        environment, cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history)\n\nCollect the values of a timestep in a NamedTuple: the state and the action of the cognitive model, and under environment the state and the observation of the environment, which the next timestep starts from, and under saved what cognitive_save_history and environment_save_history select, as map_saved arranges them.\n\nEach side has the values in its own names: observation is the observation as the cognitive model receives it and environment_observation as the environment gave it, and action is the action as the cognitive model gave it and environment_action as the environment receives it. The two of a pair differ when a NameMap selects or renames them. Used by simulate_timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_timestep_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.environment_values-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"environment_values(state, observation)\n\nReturn the values of a timestep that the environment continues from, its state and its observation, in a NamedTuple. Used by collect_timestep_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.environment_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.get_timestep_size-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"get_timestep_size(timestep_sizes, t)\n\nReturn the size of timestep t. A vector has one size for each timestep, and anything else is the size of all of them. Used on each timestep, by the simulation loop and by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.get_timestep_size"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_all_storage-Tuple{Nothing, Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"initialize_all_storage(initial_timestep_values, saved, n_steps)\n\nCreate the vectors for saving n_steps timesteps, arranged as map_saved arranges them, and typed by saved, what the first timestep saved. When initial_timestep_values isn't nothing, each vector starts with what it has for timestep 0, from collect_initial_timestep_values. Used by simulate_all_timesteps and by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_all_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.map_names-Tuple{Any, Nothing}","page":"Simulation","text":"map_names(x, name_map)\n\nReturn what the receiver gets of the outputs x: all of x without a map, and with a NameMap the outputs it takes, under the names it gives them. Used on each timestep of a simulation, by simulate_timestep, and for timestep 0 by collect_initial_timestep_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.map_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.map_saved-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Vararg{NamedTuple, N}}} where {F, N}","page":"Simulation","text":"map_saved(f, saved...)\n\nApply f to each kind of saved value in saved, and return the results arranged in the same way: the cognitive model's states, observations, actions and action_distributions, and under environment the environment's states, observations, actions and observation_distributions, as simulate returns them. What a fitted session without an environment saves has no environment, and is arranged without one. With several, f takes the values of the same kind from each. Used to create, fill and shorten the vectors of the storage of a simulation and of a fitted session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.map_saved"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.merge_default_parameters-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"merge_default_parameters(model, parameters)\n\nReturn parameters with the values from default_parameters of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, for the parameters it doesn't have, if model provides defaults for them. Used by simulate, InstantiatedModel and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.merge_default_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.sample_output-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"sample_output(rng, output_distribution)\n\nSample an output from what a model returned, using rand. Each output in a NamedTuple is sampled on its own, so a NamedTuple inside it gives a NamedTuple of samples, and an entry that is NoValue is kept. A number, a string or a symbol is a value and not something to sample from, and throws an ArgumentError which names Deterministic. Used on each timestep of a simulation, by simulate_timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.sample_output"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.saved_trajectories-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"saved_trajectories(storage, timestep_sizes)\n\nReturn the saved trajectories of a simulation as simulate returns them: the vectors in storage, with the cognitive model's under states, observations, actions and action_distributions and the environment's under environment, and then timestep_sizes as given. Used by simulate, simulate! and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saved_trajectories"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate_all_timesteps-NTuple{15, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate_all_timesteps(previous_timestep_values, who_starts, cognitive_model,\n                       environment, cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters,\n                       cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history,\n                       observation_map, action_map, rng, timestep_sizes, n_steps,\n                       save_initial_timestep_values::Val, check_stopping_criterion)\n\nSimulate up to n_steps timesteps starting from previous_timestep_values, and return the vectors with what the simulated timesteps saved, followed by the values of the last one. When check_stopping_criterion is true, the simulation stops before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of either model is met, and none can be simulated if it is met already. When save_initial_timestep_values is Val(true), each vector starts with what is saved from before the first timestep, as timestep 0.\n\nprevious_timestep_values and the values returned are those of a timestep: the state and the action of the cognitive model, and under environment the state and the observation of the environment, each in its own names. The vectors are arranged as map_saved arranges them, with what cognitive_save_history and environment_save_history select.\n\nsimulate! continues from the values of the last timestep instead of from what was saved, because what is saved of a state can be a selection or a copy of it, and because the next simulation can change a state in place. Used by simulate and simulate!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate_all_timesteps"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate_remaining_timesteps-NTuple{16, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate_remaining_timesteps(storage, timestep_values, who_starts, cognitive_model,\n                             environment, cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters,\n                             cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history,\n                             observation_map, action_map, rng, timestep_sizes, n_steps,\n                             offset, check_stopping_criterion)\n\nSimulate the timesteps after the first, and return the vectors with what the simulated timesteps saved, followed by the values of the last one. When check_stopping_criterion is true, the simulation stops before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of either model is met. offset is 1 when the vectors start with timestep 0, and 0 otherwise, so that timestep t is element t + offset. Used by simulate_all_timesteps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate_remaining_timesteps"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep-NTuple{8, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate_timestep(model, input, previous_state, previous_output, parameters,\n                  timestep_size, rng, save_history)\n\nSimulate a timestep of model on input, without the other side of a simulation, and return the output sampled from what it gave, its new state, and under saved what save_history selects of its states, inputs, outputs and output_distributions, in a NamedTuple. The input must have the names in the attributes of model. simulate and simulate! on a single input both use this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep-Tuple{AgentStarts, Vararg{Any, 11}}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate_timestep(::AgentStarts, cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_parameters,\n                  environment_parameters, cognitive_save_history,\n                  environment_save_history, observation_map, action_map, rng,\n                  timestep_size, previous)\n\nSimulate a timestep where the agent moves first, starting from previous, the values of the timestep before, and return its values, from collect_timestep_values. Used by simulate_all_timesteps and simulate_remaining_timesteps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep-Tuple{EnvironmentStarts, Vararg{Any, 11}}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate_timestep(::EnvironmentStarts, cognitive_model, environment,\n                  cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters,\n                  cognitive_save_history, environment_save_history, observation_map,\n                  action_map, rng, timestep_size, previous)\n\nSimulate a timestep where the environment moves first, starting from previous, the values of the timestep before, and return its values, from collect_timestep_values. Used by simulate_all_timesteps and simulate_remaining_timesteps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.store_timestep_values!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"store_timestep_values!(storage, saved, t)\n\nStore saved, what timestep t saved, as element t of each vector in storage, with store_value!, and return the vectors arranged in the same way, in which a vector is wider when a value didn't fit it. Used by simulate_remaining_timesteps and by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_timestep_values!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.given_name-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Simulation","text":"given_name(name)\nreceived_name(name)\n\nReturn the name an output is given under and the name it is received under, from one argument of a NameMap: the two sides of a :given => :received pair, or a single Symbol, which is received under the name it is given with. Used by the constructor of NameMap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.given_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.starting_history-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any, Any, SaveHistory}","page":"Simulation","text":"starting_history(model, state, previous_output, save_history)\n\nReturn the history that an InstantiatedModel starts with: a ModelHistory with what save_history selects of where it starts, as timestep 0, and BeforeFirstTimestep as the size of the timestep, since none has run yet. When nothing is selected, or save_history doesn't save the initial timestep values, the vectors are empty.\n\nA model hasn't received anything when it starts, so that entry has every input in the model's attributes as BeforeFirstTimestep(), and so has every output distribution. reset! starts from the same history as the constructors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.starting_history"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"Placeholder tutorial. Each file in docs/literate/ is a plain Julia script that becomes a page like this one when the documentation is built.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Fitting-with-Turing.jl","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"The Turing.jl models of the sessions on the page Fitting, what a model can add for them, and reading the trajectories they saved from a chain.\n\nPages = [\"fitting_with_turing.md\"]","title":"Fitting with Turing.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Turing.jl-models","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"","title":"Turing.jl models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Postprocessing","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"","title":"Postprocessing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Predictive-checks","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"With Turing.jl loaded, predict_session_trajectories takes a model made by turing_full_model, turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions or turing_behavioral_session, or such a model inside turing_catch_invalid_parameters, and a chain sampled from it. It returns the simulated trajectories as a FlexiChain, as session_trajectories_chain makes it. For a prior predictive check, give a chain sampled from the prior, such as sample(model, Prior(), 1000).\n\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\nsimulated = predict_session_trajectories(model, chain; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; outputs = :choice))\n\n#The simulated choices of participant P1, for every sample\nsimulated[@varname(P1.choice)]","title":"Predictive checks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Functions-that-a-model-can-implement-for-Turing.jl","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"A model can add methods of these to change how it runs when it is fitted with Turing.jl, and must when it samples anything of its own, within a timestep or when it starts. They are public, but not exported.","title":"Functions that a model can implement for Turing.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#Fitting-several-sessions","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"These are what the parts of turing_full_model are written with, beside the types and functions on the page Fitting. They are public, but not exported. In a Turing.jl model of several sessions, session i of CognitiveModeling.session_index has its variables under session[i].","title":"Fitting several sessions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_behavioral_session","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_behavioral_session(session_info::SessionInfo, cognitive_parameters)\nturing_behavioral_session(session_info::SessionInfo, cognitive_parameters,\n                          environment_parameters=(;))\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model of one session: the cognitive model in session_info goes through the timesteps of its data with cognitive_parameters, and the actions in the data are scored against the action distributions it gives. The second form is for a session with an environment model, which takes its parameters too. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nThe model is a building block for a Turing.jl model which samples the parameters, as a submodel with its own prefix: to_submodel(prefix(turing_behavioral_session(...), @varname(session)), false). turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions runs one for each of several sessions, and turing_full_model samples their parameters too.\n\nArguments\n\nsession_info::SessionInfo: the data, the models and the settings of the session, prepared once with SessionInfo.\ncognitive_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of the cognitive model, usually the random variables of the model around this one. Those it doesn't have take the values in default_parameters, if the model provides defaults for them.\nenvironment_parameters::NamedTuple=(;): the parameters of the environment model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any. A session without an environment takes none.\n\nWhat it does\n\nIn outline, the model of a session without an environment looks like this. It is not the actual code, which also checks the names and the parameters, and saves what is selected:\n\n#The state where the session starts, which a model can sample\nstate ~ to_submodel(turing_initialize_state(cognitive_model, cognitive_parameters))\n\nfor t in 1:n_timesteps\n\n    #The cognitive model gives a distribution for each action, under the prefix timestep[t]\n    output ~ to_submodel(turing_apply_timestep_model(cognitive_model, context))\n\n    #An action that is missing in the data is a latent variable with that distribution\n    choice.imputed ~ output.output_distribution.choice\nend\n\n#The actions in the data are scored once, against the distributions of their timesteps\nactions_to_condition_on ~ product_distribution(action_distributions)\n\n#The trajectories over the timesteps that the save histories select\nreturn saved_trajectories\n\nA session which includes its environment runs the environment model in the same way on each timestep, before the cognitive model when the environment starts and after it when the agent starts, and scores the observations in the data like the actions.\n\nThe states where the session starts come from turing_initialize_state of each model with the parameters, so that a starting state which is a parameter is fitted like any other, and a model can sample its start. Both models are treated the same way on each timestep: turing_apply_timestep_model of the cognitive model runs as a submodel with the prefix timestep[t], so a model which samples within a timestep has its own variables for each timestep, such as timestep[3].noise, and an environment model runs with the prefix timestep[t].environment. The outputs of each, the actions and the observations, are then taken from the data where they are recorded, and where the data has MissingValue they are imputed from what the model gave: as a latent variable with that distribution, named timestep[3].choice.imputed or timestep[3].environment.reward.imputed, or as the value itself if it is a Deterministic. Where the data has UnimputedMissingValue, or a marker for nothing happening, the models receive the marker as it is, and nothing is sampled or scored for it. The sampler must handle the type of a latent variable: a continuous value can be sampled with NUTS, a discrete one needs a sampler for discrete variables. The actions in the data are scored at the end as observed values, in one product_distribution over every action and timestep, against the distributions the cognitive model gave for them. The observations in the data are scored the same way against the distributions the environment gave, so that they inform the missing values and the environment's parameters. A Deterministic is scored as a point mass, with a log density of 0 for its value and -Inf for any other, and only a number or a Bool can be, so a value in the data that the model gives as another kind of Deterministic is an error, and so is one where the model gave nothing, NoValue. When the session doesn't include its environment, as the include_environment of session_info decides, which by default is when nothing in the data is MissingValue() and the environment's history isn't saved, the session is the same as one without an environment, with the same variables. The stopping_criterion of the models is checked as the check_stopping_criterion of session_info says: by default only after the last timestep with data, so that a fit runs every timestep and a session from prepare_for_prediction can end early.\n\nWhat it returns\n\nThe trajectories that the save histories of session_info select, in a NamedTuple with the same entries as simulate returns: states, observations, actions and action_distributions of the cognitive model, environment with the states, observations, actions and observation_distributions of the environment when the session has one, which are empty when it didn't run, and timestep_sizes. Each is a vector with one element for timestep 0, before the first timestep, and one for each timestep that ran, which returned(model, chain) gives for each sample. Timestep 0 has the previous observation and action, which are BeforeFirstTimestep unless others are given, BeforeFirstTimestep() for every distribution, and the initial states, which can depend on the parameters. Element t + 1 is then timestep t, and a SaveHistory with save_initial_timestep_values = false leaves timestep 0 out. The returned actions and observations are those the models received, with the imputed values. timestep_sizes has the size of each timestep, after BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0 when it is there, when anything is selected, and is empty otherwise. Any latent variable in a session, imputed or from a model which samples within a timestep, greatly increases the amount of estimated variables, which will generally make fitting the model much slower.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\n#The model to fit, a Rescorla-Wagner model with a softmax choice\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = parameters.initial_value)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    Pₜ = BernoulliLogit(context.parameters.inverse_temperature * (Vₜ - 0.5))\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Pₜ), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature, :initial_value),\n        states = :expected_reward,\n        inputs = :reward,\n        outputs = :choice,\n    )\nend\n\nCognitiveModeling.default_parameters(model::RescorlaWagner) = (; initial_value = 0.5)\n\n#The data of one session, where the third choice wasn't recorded, prepared once\nsession_info = SessionInfo(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0]),\n    (; choice = [true, false, MissingValue(), true]);\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward),\n)\n\n#Priors on the parameters, and the session as a submodel\n@model function fit_one_session(session_info)\n    learning_rate ~ Beta(2, 2)\n    inverse_temperature ~ LogNormal(0, 1)\n    session ~ to_submodel(\n        prefix(\n            turing_behavioral_session(session_info, (; learning_rate, inverse_temperature)),\n            @varname(session),\n        ),\n        false,\n    )\n    return session\nend\n\nchain = sample(fit_one_session(session_info), NUTS(), 1000)\n\n#The expected rewards on each timestep, for each sample\ntrajectories = returned(fit_one_session(session_info), chain)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_behavioral_session"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_catch_invalid_parameters","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_catch_invalid_parameters(model)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model which runs model, any Turing.jl model, inside a try block: an InvalidParametersError thrown by a cognitive model or an environment model then gives the parameters a log density of -Inf, so that a sampler rejects the sample instead of stopping, and the model returns nothing for that sample. Otherwise it returns what model returns. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nIt goes around the part of a model that can throw, with the parameters sampled outside it, as in\n\n@model function fit_one_session(session_info)\n    learning_rate ~ Beta(2, 2)\n    session ~ to_submodel(\n        turing_catch_invalid_parameters(\n            turing_behavioral_session(session_info, (; learning_rate)),\n        ),\n        false,\n    )\nend\n\nand turing_full_model does this with catch_invalid_parameters = true. The variables sampled inside the try block are lost when it catches an error, so around a whole model a sampler can't start when its first draw is rejected. Nothing in the package catches the error unless this is used, so a sampler stops with it otherwise. Not every automatic differentiation backend can differentiate a model with it, as the page on automatic differentiation says.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_catch_invalid_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_full_model","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                  environment_parameters_model=NoParameters(),\n                  downstream_model=NoDownstreamModel(),\n                  fixed_cognitive_parameters=(;), fixed_environment_parameters=(;),\n                  catch_invalid_parameters=false, parallelization=SerialSessions(),\n                  include_submodel_prefixes=false)\n\nCreate the Turing.jl model which fits a cognitive model to several sessions, in three parts: cognitive_parameters_model gives or samples the cognitive parameters of every session, and environment_parameters_model those of the environment model, the sessions in sessions_info run with them as turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions, and downstream_model can do something with the trajectories they saved, such as a regression of a measurement on a state. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_parameters_model: the session parameter model of the cognitive model, which gives or estimates its parameters for every session: a subtype of AbstractSessionParametersModel, such as IndependentSessionParameters with a prior for each parameter.\nsessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo: the sessions, prepared once with MultipleSessionsInfo.\nenvironment_parameters_model=NoParameters(): the session parameter model of the environment model of the sessions, when they have one. By default the environment's parameters take its default values, and FixedSessionParameters gives others. When they are estimated, every session includes its environment, also those with nothing to impute, so that the observations in the data inform them, which is an error for a session prepared with include_environment = false. Sessions without an environment take no parameters.\ndownstream_model=NoDownstreamModel(): a final submodel which is run after the sessions, such as for correlations with other kinds of data: a subtype of AbstractDownstreamModel. The default samples nothing.\nfixed_cognitive_parameters::NamedTuple=(;) and fixed_environment_parameters::NamedTuple=(;): values which every session gets for the parameters they name, such as (; initial_value = 0.3), while the session parameter models give the others. A parameter is either fixed here or given by its session parameter model, and a fixed parameter must be one in the model's attributes.\ncatch_invalid_parameters::Bool=false: whether an InvalidParametersError in a session rejects the sample instead of stopping the sampler, by running the sessions inside turing_catch_invalid_parameters. If the first draw is rejected and the downstream model has variables of its own, give the sampler valid initial_params.\nparallelization=SerialSessions(): whether the sessions run over the threads Julia was started with, with ParallelSessions(). The model must then be marked with setthreadsafe(model, true) from DynamicPPL before it is sampled: without the mark, a threaded model has a wrong log density with no error, so the sessions throw an error instead.\ninclude_submodel_prefixes::Bool=false: whether the variables of the two session parameter models and of the downstream model are named under cognitive_parameters_model, environment_parameters_model and downstream_model. Without the prefixes, the names are shorter, but a variable of the same name in two of them is one variable, which sample refuses with an error, and which a log density gives one value with both priors counted. A warning says so when the model is made, when the cognitive model and the environment model have a parameter of the same name and both session parameter models sample.\n\nThe variables of a session parameter model are named as it names them, or under the session parameter model's name with include_submodel_prefixes = true. With IndependentSessionParameters, each parameter is a vector with a value for each session, such as learning_rate.session, so the learning rate of the third session is learning_rate.session[3]. With GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters, each parameter of a group-level distribution is a vector with a value for each group, named after its prior, such as learning_rate.mean.group and learning_rate.sd.group, and the values of the sessions before the transformation are learning_rate.standardized, or learning_rate.session for a parameter that is centered. With HierarchicalRegression, the intercept of a parameter is learning_rate.intercept, and the coefficient of a predictor is under its column, such as learning_rate.age.coefficient, or under its column and its level for a categorical predictor, such as learning_rate.diagnosis_clinical.coefficient. A random intercept has its variables under its column: its standard deviation, learning_rate.participant.sd, which is a vector with a value for each group when it is grouped, and a standardized value for each level, learning_rate.participant.standardized, or the deviation of each level, learning_rate.participant.deviation, when it is centered. The deviations of the sessions have the same variables under session_deviation, such as learning_rate.session_deviation.sd. A part of a CompositeSessionParameters that is given with a name has its variables under that name, such as noise.bias.session. The variables of the sessions are under session[i], and those of the downstream model as it names them, or under downstream_model with the prefixes.\n\nThe model returns a NamedTuple with cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters, saved_behavioral_trajectories and downstream_values: the cognitive parameters and the environment parameters of every session as the session parameter models gave them, the trajectories each session saved, as a vector of what turing_behavioral_session returns, and what the downstream model returned. returned(model, chain) gives them for each sample, so a fitted state is read from the chain without simulating again.\n\nSaving states adds a cost to gradient calculations. Without a downstream model that needs them, a fit is fastest with sessions that save nothing, and the trajectories are then read from the chain afterwards with session_trajectories_chain, or with returned on the model of a copy of the sessions that saves them, MultipleSessionsInfo(sessions_info; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward)), since the two models have the same variables. A warning says so when the sessions save anything and there is no downstream model.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\n#The data of two sessions, as a table with one vector for each column\ntable = (;\n    id = [\"S1\", \"S1\", \"S1\", \"S2\", \"S2\", \"S2\"],\n    reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0],\n    choice = [true, false, true, false, missing, true],\n)\n\n#The sessions, with the RescorlaWagner model of turing_behavioral_session\nsessions_info = MultipleSessionsInfo(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    table;\n    observation_columns = :reward,\n    action_columns = :choice,\n    session_columns = :id,\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward),\n)\n\n#Each session has its own parameters, from the same priors\ncognitive_parameters_model = IndependentSessionParameters((;\n    learning_rate = Beta(2, 2),\n    inverse_temperature = LogNormal(0, 1),\n))\n\nmodel = turing_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, sessions_info)\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\n\n#The parameters and the expected rewards of every session, for each sample\nresults = returned(model, chain)\nresults[1].cognitive_parameters.learning_rate\nresults[1].saved_behavioral_trajectories[2].states\n\n#The same fit without saving, and the states read from the chain afterwards\nsessions_without_saving = MultipleSessionsInfo(\n    sessions_info; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(),\n)\nunsaved = turing_full_model(cognitive_parameters_model, sessions_without_saving)\nchain = sample(unsaved, NUTS(), 1000)\nresults = returned(model, chain)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_full_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions(sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo,\n                                    cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters=(;);\n                                    parallelization=SerialSessions())\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model which runs turing_behavioral_session for every session in sessions_info, each with its own cognitive parameters, as a submodel with the prefix session[i], so that a latent variable of the third session is named like session[3].timestep[2].choice.imputed. Which session i is stands in sessions_info.session_ids[i], and session_index gives i for an identifier. Environment parameters can only be given when the sessions have an environment model. With parallelization = ParallelSessions(), the sessions run over the threads Julia was started with. The model that is evaluated, which is the outermost one, must then be marked with setthreadsafe(model, true) from DynamicPPL, since the log density of a threaded model that isn't is wrong with no error; the sessions throw an error when it isn't marked. See parallelization in turing_full_model. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\ncognitive_parameters is a NamedTuple with one vector for each parameter, with one value for every session in the order of sessions_info, which is what a session parameter model returns, see turing_session_parameters_model, and environment_parameters is in the same form. It returns a vector with what each session returns, in the same order. The model is a building block for a Turing.jl model which samples the parameters of the sessions in some way of its own, as a submodel without a prefix: to_submodel(turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions(sessions_info, parameters), false). turing_full_model does this with session parameter models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_multiple_behavioral_sessions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.session_parameters_chain","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"session_parameters_chain(results, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                         iter_indices=1:size(results, 1),\n                         chain_indices=1:size(results, 2))\nsession_parameters_chain(model, chain)\n\nReturn the parameters of every fitted session, for every sample, as a FlexiChain with the same samples as the fit, named after the session: P1.learning_rate for a parameter of the cognitive model, and P1.environment.mean_reward for one of the environment model. This needs FlexiChains.jl to be loaded, which Turing.jl loads.\n\nresults is a matrix with what turing_full_model returns for each sample, as returned(model, chain) gives it, with the MultipleSessionsInfo of the model. The parameters are those the session parameter models gave each session, so a session parameter model other than IndependentSessionParameters gives parameters that aren't in the chain itself, such as one value for every session from IdenticalSessionParameters, and values from FixedSessionParameters that were never sampled. The second form, in the Turing extension, takes a model made by turing_full_model and a chain sampled from it, and numbers the samples as the chain does. The result merges with the chain of session_trajectories_chain, which has the same samples, with merge.\n\nExamples\n\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\nparameters = session_parameters_chain(model, chain)\n\n#The learning rate of participant P1, for every sample\nparameters[@varname(P1.learning_rate)]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_parameters_chain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.session_trajectories_chain","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"session_trajectories_chain(results, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo;\n                           iter_indices=1:size(results, 1),\n                           chain_indices=1:size(results, 2))\nsession_trajectories_chain(results; iter_indices=1:size(results, 1),\n                           chain_indices=1:size(results, 2))\nsession_trajectories_chain(model, chain; cognitive_save_history=SaveHistory(),\n                           environment_save_history=SaveHistory())\n\nReturn the trajectories that fitted sessions saved, for every sample, as a FlexiChain from FlexiChains.jl with the same samples as the fit, so that they can be summarized and plotted like the chain itself. The trajectories of a session are what changes over its timesteps: the states, the observations and the actions of its models, and the distributions they gave. This needs FlexiChains.jl to be loaded, which Turing.jl loads. The names of the entries are the VarNames that FlexiChains.jl uses, written as @varname(P1.expected_value) with FlexiChains.jl 0.6, which Turing.jl uses, and as @vn(P1.expected_value) with 0.7.\n\nresults is a matrix with one element for each sample, iterations down and chains across, as returned(model, chain) gives it in Turing.jl. Its elements are what one of three models returns:\n\nturing_full_model, given with its MultipleSessionsInfo. The entries of each session are named after its symbol name, such as P1.expected_value. The parameters of the sessions are a chain of their own, see session_parameters_chain, and what a downstream model returned isn't included.\nturing_multiple_behavioral_sessions, given with its MultipleSessionsInfo, named in the same way.\nturing_behavioral_session, given without a session info, with no symbol name of a session in the names, such as expected_value.\n\nIn a model of one's own around these, that model must return what they return, for returned to give it.\n\nThe trajectories each session saved, as its SaveHistorys selected, are one entry for each name, with the session's symbol name first. The cognitive model's values are named after the attribute alone, since a model uses a name for one attribute only: a state as P1.expected_value, an observation as P1.reward and an action as P1.choice. An action distribution is named like its action, so it is under its kind, as P1.action_distributions.choice. The environment's values are under environment, in the same way: P1.environment.level for a state, P1.environment.reward for an observation it gave, P1.environment.choice for an action it received and P1.environment.observation_distributions.reward. Each entry holds a vector over the timesteps for every sample, which starts with timestep 0 when the initial timestep values are saved, so that element t + 1 is timestep t, as everywhere in the package. The observations, the actions and the distributions of timestep 0 are BeforeFirstTimestep, so a summary of those entries needs timestep 0 left out. The symbol name of a session with several session columns joins the name and the value of each column, as in id_S1__block_2, as CognitiveModeling.session_id_to_symbol gives it. iter_indices and chain_indices number the samples as the chain that was fitted does.\n\nThe third form, in the Turing extension, takes a model made by one of the three functions, or such a model inside turing_catch_invalid_parameters, and a chain sampled from it. It makes a copy of the model whose sessions save what the two save histories select, gives it to returned, and makes the chain of the results, numbered as chain is. A fit is fastest when its sessions save nothing, and their trajectories are then read afterwards in this way. Selecting anything in environment_save_history makes every session run its environment, see SessionInfo.\n\nExamples\n\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\nsession_trajectories = session_trajectories_chain(model, chain; cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_value))\n\n#The expected values of participant P2 over the timesteps, for every sample\nsession_trajectories[@varname(P2.expected_value)]\n\n#The parameters of the sessions, in the same chain\neverything = merge(session_trajectories, session_parameters_chain(model, chain))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_trajectories_chain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_apply_timestep_model","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_apply_timestep_model(model::AbstractTimestepModel, context::ModelContext)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model for a single timestep of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, to be used as a submodel, which returns a ModelOutput. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default calls apply_timestep_model. A model which samples within a timestep needs to add a method, however; or if any other changes are desired within Turing. It must sample with ~ instead of from context.rng. Otherwise the model has a different log density each time it is evaluated, with no error. The same holds for an environment model, which fitting uses to fill in observations that are MissingValue in the data: what it samples itself, such as a change of its state, must be sampled with ~, while fitting samples the observations from what it returns.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\nstruct NoisyRescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nTuring.@model function CognitiveModeling.turing_apply_timestep_model(\n        model::NoisyRescorlaWagner,\n        context::ModelContext,\n    )\n\n    ## Unpack the context ##\n    #Reward observed on this timestep\n    r = context.input.reward\n    #Expected reward from the previous state\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    #Learning rate parameter\n    α = context.parameters.learning_rate\n\n    ## Update state ##\n    #Sample the noise in the update\n    ϵ ~ Normal(0, context.parameters.noise)\n    #Update expected reward using the Rescorla-Wagner rule\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + α * (r - V₋₁) + ϵ\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = BernoulliLogit(Vₜ - 0.5)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_apply_timestep_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_initialize_state","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_initialize_state(model::AbstractTimestepModel, parameters)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model which gives the state of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, before the first timestep, to be used as a submodel, as initialize_state does. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default calls initialize_state, which is enough for most models, since a starting state that is fitted is simply one that is copied from a parameter there: a fitted session runs it with the parameters of each evaluation, so a prior on the parameter is a prior on the starting state. A model which samples something of its own when it initializes needs to add a method, and sample it with ~, as for turing_apply_timestep_model. Its variables are named under initial_cognitive_state, or initial_environment_state for an environment model.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\nstruct UncertainStart <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nTuring.@model function CognitiveModeling.turing_initialize_state(\n        model::UncertainStart,\n        parameters,\n    )\n\n    #Sample where the expected reward starts, around the parameter for it\n    V₀ ~ Normal(parameters.initial_value, parameters.initial_noise)\n\n    return (; expected_reward = V₀)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_initialize_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_session_parameters_model","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_session_parameters_model(parameters_model, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo)\n\nCreate the Turing.jl model of parameters_model, a session parameter model, which gives or samples the parameters of every session in sessions_info, for the cognitive model or the environment model of a turing_full_model. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nThe model must return a NamedTuple with one entry for each parameter, named as the model names it, and a vector in each with one value for every session, in the order of the sessions. Each subtype of AbstractSessionParametersModel that comes with CognitiveModeling.jl has a method.\n\nImplementation\n\nA session parameter model of another kind, such as a regression, is a subtype of AbstractSessionParametersModel with a method of this function. It can read n_sessions and the identifiers of the sessions from sessions_info, the columns of the table that the sessions were split from, when there is one, with CognitiveModeling.session_column_values, such as the covariates of a regression, and anything else from its own fields. What it reads from the table is best read once, in a method of CognitiveModeling.prepare_parameters_model. The full model treats it as one that samples, so that with it the environment of every session runs, and the observations in the data inform the environment's parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_session_parameters_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/fitting_with_turing/#CognitiveModeling.turing_downstream_model","page":"Fitting with Turing.jl","text":"turing_downstream_model(downstream_model, saved_behavioral_trajectories,\n                        cognitive_parameters, sessions_info::MultipleSessionsInfo)\n\nCreate the Turing.jl model of downstream_model, the final submodel of a turing_full_model, which is run after the sessions, with the trajectories the sessions saved, the cognitive parameters of every session as the session parameter model returned them, and the sessions' sessions_info. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nThe model can return anything, which the full model returns as its downstream_values. NoDownstreamModel is the default, with a method which samples nothing and returns nothing.\n\nImplementation\n\nA downstream model is a subtype of AbstractDownstreamModel with a method of this function, which can ignore any of the arguments. A regression of some measurement on a state of the cognitive model, for instance, reads the state from saved_behavioral_trajectories, a vector with the saved trajectories of each session as turing_behavioral_session returns them, so the cognitive_save_history of the sessions must select that state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_downstream_model"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_sequential_sampling_models/#Models-from-SequentialSamplingModels.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from SequentialSamplingModels.jl as cognitive models","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use the sequential sampling models of SequentialSamplingModels.jl, such as the drift diffusion model, as what a cognitive model samples its actions from, so that it gives both a choice and a response time.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Models from SequentialSamplingModels.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Internals-of-fitting","page":"Fitting","text":"Pages = [\"fitting.md\"]","title":"Internals of fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Sessions","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Session-parameter-models","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Session parameter models"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Preparing-a-full-model","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Preparing a full model"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Preparing-sessions","page":"Fitting","text":"What runs once, when the sessions and the parts of a full model are prepared.","title":"Preparing sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Preparing-the-data-of-a-session","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Preparing the data of a session"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Splitting-a-table-into-sessions","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Splitting a table into sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Including-the-environment-in-a-session,-or-leaving-it-out","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Including the environment in a session, or leaving it out"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Checking-the-identifiers-of-the-sessions","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Checking the identifiers of the sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Reading-the-columns-that-a-session-parameter-model-uses","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Reading the columns that a session parameter model uses"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Running-sessions","page":"Fitting","text":"What an inference backend's model of the sessions calls.","title":"Running sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Reading-what-a-session-is-set-up-with","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Reading what a session is set up with"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Reading-the-data-of-one-timestep","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Reading the data of one timestep"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Values-to-condition-on","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Values to condition on"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Saving-what-a-fitted-session-returns","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Saving what a fitted session returns"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Displaying-sessions","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Displaying sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/fitting/#Reading-tables-with-Tables.jl","page":"Fitting","text":"","title":"Reading tables with Tables.jl"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_session-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"prepare_session(cognitive_model, observations, actions; who_starts, <keyword arguments>)\n\nReturn the SessionInfo of one session, with the checks of the data and the names, the missing values in the data marked, whether the environment runs, and what is scored and returned prepared, as its constructor describes. Used by SessionInfo and MultipleSessionsInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_session"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.collect_parameters_in_session_order-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"collect_parameters_in_session_order(parameters_by_session, session_ids)\n\nReturn the parameters of each session in parameters_by_session, as FixedParametersBySession holds them, as one vector for each parameter with a value for every session, in the order of session_ids, with the parameters of each session found by parameters_of_session. A session with other parameters than the first is an error. Parameters that are already in the order of the sessions are returned as they are, after checking that each vector has a value for every session. Used by prepare_parameters_model, and by the models of FixedParametersBySession in the inference backends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_parameters_in_session_order"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.has_vector_for_each_parameter-Tuple{Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"has_vector_for_each_parameter(parameters_by_session)\n\nReturn whether parameters_by_session, as a FixedParametersBySession holds it, gives the parameters in the order of the sessions, as a NamedTuple with one vector for each parameter, and not by the sessions' symbol names or identifiers. Used by parameter_names and collect_parameters_in_session_order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_vector_for_each_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.parameters_of_session-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"parameters_of_session(parameters_by_session, session_id)\n\nReturn the parameters of the session with the identifier session_id in parameters_by_session: the entry under the session's symbol name, as session_id_to_symbol gives it, in a NamedTuple, and the entry under the identifier itself in a dictionary. A session without parameters is an error. Used by collect_parameters_in_session_order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.parameters_of_session"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.ConstructorOf","page":"Fitting","text":"ConstructorOf{T}()\n\nA function which makes an object of the type T from its arguments, so that ConstructorOf{Normal}()(0, 1) is Normal(0, 1). Used by HierarchicalParameter for a group-level distribution that is given as a type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ConstructorOf"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_is_hierarchical_parameter-Tuple{Symbol, HierarchicalParameter}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_is_hierarchical_parameter(name, hierarchical_parameter)\n\nThrow an informative error if what a GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters was given for the parameter name isn't a HierarchicalParameter. Used by the constructor of GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_is_hierarchical_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.as_random_intercept-Tuple{RandomIntercept}","page":"Fitting","text":"as_random_intercept(random_intercept)\n\nReturn random_intercept as a RandomIntercept: itself when it is one, and otherwise one with the default settings whose standard deviation has it as its prior. Used by the constructor of RegressionParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_random_intercept"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.build_contrast_columns-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Symbol, RegressionParameter, Symbol}","page":"Fitting","text":"build_contrast_columns(sessions_info, column, regression_parameter, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the columns of the categorical predictor column in the regression of the parameter parameter_name, in a NamedTuple with names, columns and reference_level: for each level other than the reference level, in the order of their first session, the name of its coefficient, made from the column and the symbol name of the level, as in diagnosis_clinical, and a vector with 1 for the sessions of that level and 0 for the others. With sum_to_zero_coding, the sessions of the reference level have -1 in every column. The reference level is the one in reference_levels of regression_parameter, or the level of the first session. It is an error when the column has one level only, and when the reference level isn't a value of the column. Used by build_regression_design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_contrast_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.build_regression_design-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, RegressionParameter, Symbol}","page":"Fitting","text":"build_regression_design(sessions_info, regression_parameter, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the design of the regression of the parameter parameter_name for the sessions in sessions_info, as HierarchicalRegression keeps it in its field designs: the prior and the column of each coefficient, the means that were subtracted, the reference levels, and the levels of the sessions for each random intercept and for the deviations of the sessions. Used by prepare_parameters_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_regression_design"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_has_priors-Tuple{NamedTuple, String}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_has_priors(predictors, keyword)\n\nThrow an informative error if a column in predictors, a NamedTuple from each column to the prior of its coefficient, has nothing as its prior. keyword is the name of the keyword argument they were given with. Used by the constructor of RegressionParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_has_priors"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_is_regression_parameter-Tuple{Symbol, RegressionParameter}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_is_regression_parameter(name, regression_parameter)\n\nThrow an informative error if what a HierarchicalRegression was given for the parameter name isn't a RegressionParameter. Used by the constructor of HierarchicalRegression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_is_regression_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_unique_term_names-Tuple{Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_unique_term_names(term_names)\n\nThrow an informative error if two terms of a regression have the same name in term_names, a tuple or a vector with intercept, session_deviation, the name of each coefficient and the column of each random intercept, since the variables of a term are named under its name. Used by the constructor of RegressionParameter, for the columns, and by build_regression_design, for the coefficients of the levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_unique_term_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.find_groups_of_levels-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Tuple{}, Any, Any, String}","page":"Fitting","text":"find_groups_of_levels(sessions_info, group_by, level_of_each_session,\n                      level_symbol_names, description)\n\nReturn the groups of the levels of a random intercept by the values of the columns in group_by, a tuple of names, so that each group has a standard deviation of its own: a NamedTuple with the group of every level, group_of_each_level, the number of groups, n_groups, and the symbol name of each group, group_symbol_names, as group_sessions gives them for the sessions. Without columns it returns nothing, for one standard deviation. A level whose sessions are in more than one group is an error. description says what is grouped, for the errors, such as \"the random intercepts of learning_rate for participant\". Used by find_random_intercept_levels and find_session_deviation_levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.find_groups_of_levels"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.find_random_intercept_levels-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Symbol, RandomIntercept, Symbol}","page":"Fitting","text":"find_random_intercept_levels(sessions_info, column, random_intercept, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the levels of the random intercept for column in the regression of the parameter parameter_name, as a NamedTuple with the level of every session, level_of_each_session, the number of levels, n_levels, the symbol name of each level, level_symbol_names, and groups, from find_groups_of_levels. The levels are the values of the column, numbered in the order of their first session. Used by build_regression_design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.find_random_intercept_levels"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.find_session_deviation_levels-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Nothing, Symbol}","page":"Fitting","text":"find_session_deviation_levels(sessions_info, session_deviation, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the levels of the deviations of the sessions in the regression of the parameter parameter_name, in the form of find_random_intercept_levels, where every session is a level of its own, named by its symbol name, or nothing when session_deviation is nothing. Used by build_regression_design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.find_session_deviation_levels"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.pair_predictors_with_priors-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any, String}","page":"Fitting","text":"pair_predictors_with_priors(predictors, coefficient_prior, keyword)\n\nReturn the predictors of a RegressionParameter as a NamedTuple from each column to the prior of its coefficient: a NamedTuple as it is, and columns that are given without priors, as one name or several in a tuple or a vector, each with coefficient_prior, which is an error when it is nothing. keyword is the name of the keyword argument they were given with, for the error. Used by the constructor of RegressionParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.pair_predictors_with_priors"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.read_predictor_values-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Symbol, Symbol}","page":"Fitting","text":"read_predictor_values(sessions_info, column, parameter_name)\n\nReturn the value of the continuous predictor column in every session, as a vector of Float64, for the regression of the parameter parameter_name. A value that isn't a number is an error. Used by build_regression_design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.read_predictor_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.add_part_without_name-Tuple{CompositeSessionParameters, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"add_part_without_name(parameters_model, part)\n\nReturn parameters_model with part added to it as a part without a name: a CompositeSessionParameters with its own parts, which keep their names, and then part, or a new CompositeSessionParameters of parameters_model and part for any other session parameter model. Used by with_fixed_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.add_part_without_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_parts_give_different_parameters-Tuple{Tuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_parts_give_different_parameters(parts)\n\nThrow an informative error if two of parts, the parts of a CompositeSessionParameters, give the same parameter, for the parts whose parameter_names are known. Used by the constructor of CompositeSessionParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_parts_give_different_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.merge_parameters_of_parts-Tuple{NamedTuple, NamedTuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"merge_parameters_of_parts(parameters, other_parameters)\n\nReturn the parameters that two parts of a CompositeSessionParameters give, each a NamedTuple with one vector for each parameter, merged into one, after checking that they give different parameters, which matters for the parts whose names weren't known when the model was made. Used by the models of CompositeSessionParameters in the inference backends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.merge_parameters_of_parts"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.part_names-Union{Tuple{CompositeSessionParameters{Tparts, Tpart_names}}, Tuple{Tpart_names}, Tuple{Tparts}} where {Tparts, Tpart_names}","page":"Fitting","text":"part_names(parameters_model)\n\nReturn the name of each part of parameters_model, a CompositeSessionParameters, in a tuple in the order of its parts, with nothing for a part given without a name. Used by the models of CompositeSessionParameters in the inference backends, which name the variables of a part under its name, and by prepare_parameters_model and add_part_without_name, which keep the names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.part_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_fixed_parameter_names-Tuple{NamedTuple, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, String}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_fixed_parameter_names(fixed_parameters, model, keyword)\n\nThrow an informative error if fixed_parameters, the values given with the keyword argument keyword, name a parameter that isn't in the attributes of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, from attribute_names, or if they are given for sessions without an environment, where model is NoEnvironment(). Used by with_fixed_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_fixed_parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_given_parameters-Tuple{AbstractSessionParametersModel, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Bool}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_given_parameters(parameters_model, model, model_runs)\n\nThrow an informative error if parameters_model, a session parameter model, gives a parameter that isn't in the attributes of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, from attribute_names, or, when model_runs is true, doesn't give one in its attributes that has no default value in default_parameters. For sessions without an environment, model is NoEnvironment(), and any parameter given is an error. Nothing is checked when parameter_names doesn't know the parameters. Used by prepare_full_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_given_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_is_session_parameters_model-Tuple{AbstractSessionParametersModel}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_is_session_parameters_model(parameters_model)\ncheck_is_downstream_model(downstream_model)\n\nThrow an informative error if parameters_model isn't a subtype of AbstractSessionParametersModel, such as the priors on their own, or if downstream_model isn't a subtype of AbstractDownstreamModel. Used by prepare_full_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_is_session_parameters_model"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.with_fixed_parameters-Tuple{Any, NamedTuple, Any, String}","page":"Fitting","text":"with_fixed_parameters(parameters_model, fixed_parameters, model, keyword)\n\nReturn parameters_model, a session parameter model, combined with the values in fixed_parameters, which every session gets for the parameters they name: a CompositeSessionParameters of its parts, which keep their names, and a FixedSessionParameters without a name, or parameters_model itself when no values are fixed. It is an error when a fixed parameter isn't in the attributes of model, and when parameters_model gives it too, which is checked here when parameter_names knows its parameters, and when the full model runs otherwise. keyword is the name of the keyword argument, for the errors. Used by prepare_full_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.with_fixed_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_data_fits-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, NamedTuple, NamedTuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_data_fits(cognitive_model, observations, actions)\n\nThrow an informative error if the data of a session isn't a NamedTuple of vectors for the observations and one for the actions, doesn't have the observations and the actions in the cognitive model's attributes, from attribute_names, or has columns of different lengths, and return the number of timesteps otherwise. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_data_fits"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_environment_fits-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, NoEnvironment, NamedTuple, Any, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_environment_fits(cognitive_model, environment, observations)\n\nThrow an informative error if a session can't run with environment: without one, the observations in the data can't have a MissingValue, since nothing imputes it, and with one, its attributes must have the same observations and actions as those of cognitive_model. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_environment_fits"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.collect_values_to_condition_on-Union{Tuple{NamedTuple{names}}, Tuple{names}} where names","page":"Fitting","text":"collect_values_to_condition_on(columns)\n\nReturn the values to condition on of each action or observation in columns, a NamedTuple with one vector per name, as a NamedTuple of vectors in the order of the timesteps. Fitting scores these against the distributions the model gave on the same timesteps: the actions against the cognitive model's, and the observations against the environment's. Each vector has the element type of its column without MissingValue and the other markers, so that a session without values to condition on for a name gets an empty vector of the same type as other sessions, which scores nothing. A name whose column can only hold those markers is left out, which is decided by its type, so that it is the same for every session of a table. Used by SessionInfo, which keeps the results in its fields actions_to_condition_on and observations_to_condition_on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_values_to_condition_on"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.column_can_hold-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"column_can_hold(column, value)\n\nReturn whether column can hold value, by its element type, or holds a value which is equal to it without having its type, such as -1.0 for -1, as isequal compares them. Used by mark_missing_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.column_can_hold"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.first_timestep_to_check_stopping-Tuple{Nothing, NamedTuple, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"first_timestep_to_check_stopping(check_stopping_criterion, observations, actions,\n                                 n_timesteps)\n\nReturn the first timestep before which a session checks the stopping_criterion of its models, from the check_stopping_criterion of SessionInfo: the timestep after the last one with data when it is nothing, which is 1 when every value is MissingValue, 1 when it is true, and n_timesteps + 1 when it is false, so that no timestep is checked. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.first_timestep_to_check_stopping"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.is_missing_data_value-Tuple{Any, Tuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"is_missing_data_value(value, missing_values)\n\nReturn whether value is missing in the data: MissingValue itself, or equal to one of missing_values, as isequal compares them. Used by build_column, for a value which is built from several columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.is_missing_data_value"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.map_to_environment_outputs-Tuple{Any, NoEnvironment, Any, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"map_to_environment_outputs(values, environment, observation_map, value_if_not_passed_on)\n\nReturn values, a NamedTuple in the names under which the cognitive model receives the observations, as environment gives them: under each output name in the environment's attributes, the value that observation_map passes on from it, or value_if_not_passed_on for an output that the map doesn't pass on. Without a map the names are the same. Without an environment values is returned as it is. Used by SessionInfo for the environment's data, with a column of MissingValue for each output that isn't passed on, so that it is imputed, and for the observation the environment starts from, with BeforeFirstTimestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.map_to_environment_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.mark_missing_values-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple, Bool}","page":"Fitting","text":"mark_missing_values(columns, missing_values, impute)\n\nReturn columns, a NamedTuple with one vector per name, or a single vector, with every value which is equal to one of missing_values, as isequal compares them, replaced by the marker for a missing value: MissingValue when impute is true. When it is false, the marker is UnimputedMissingValue, which every MissingValue() that is in the data already becomes too.\n\nA column gets the type of the marker in its element type when its element type allows one of the values, whether it holds one or not, so that sessions whose columns have the same types keep them, or when it holds a value equal to one of them of another type, such as -1.0 for -1. Any other column is kept as it is, after narrow_element_type. Anything that isn't a NamedTuple of vectors is returned as it is, for check_data_fits to report. Used by SessionInfo on the observations and the actions, and by build_column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.mark_missing_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.missing_value_marker-Tuple{Bool}","page":"Fitting","text":"missing_value_marker(impute)\n\nReturn the marker which the missing values in the data of a session become: MissingValue when they are imputed, and UnimputedMissingValue when impute is false. Used by mark_missing_values and build_column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.missing_value_marker"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.narrow_element_type-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Fitting","text":"narrow_element_type(column)\n\nReturn column with the types it holds as its element type, when its element type is Any or another abstract type that isn't a Union, and column itself otherwise. A vector written as [true, MissingValue(), true] has the element type Any, since only missing is promoted to a Union, and an Any in the data makes every timestep run with its types unknown. Used by mark_missing_values and build_column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.narrow_element_type"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.value_passed_on-Tuple{Any, Any, Nothing, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"value_passed_on(values, name, observation_map, value_if_not_passed_on)\n\nReturn the value in values that the environment's output name is received as: under the same name without a map, under the name observation_map gives it, or value_if_not_passed_on when the map doesn't pass it on. Used by map_to_environment_outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.value_passed_on"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.build_column-Tuple{NamedTuple, Symbol, Any, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"build_column(table, specification, missing_values, impute)\n\nReturn the column of an attribute for every row of table, as columns_used describes the specification, with the values that are missing replaced by their marker, as mark_missing_values does: the column itself, or one built row by row from several, where a missing value in any of them makes the whole value of the row missing. The column is made for the whole table, so that every session gets the same element type. Used by build_columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_column"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.build_columns-Tuple{NamedTuple, NamedTuple, Any, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"build_columns(table, columns, missing_values, impute)\n\nReturn the columns in columns, a NamedTuple from the name the model uses to the specification of the column in table, for every row of the table, as a NamedTuple with the model's names, each built with build_column. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_table-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_table(table, columns)\n\nThrow an informative error if table, a NamedTuple, doesn't have vectors of one length, or doesn't have every column in columns. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_table"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.column_entry-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Fitting","text":"column_entry(entry)\n\nReturn the name the model uses for an attribute and the specification of its column, as a pair, from one entry of the columns of a kind. An entry is a name, for a column that is named as the model names the attribute, or a pair from the specification of the column to the model's name: :outcome => :reward for a column with another name, (:choicecol, :rtcol) => :ssm_action or (; choice = :choicecol, rt = :rtcol) => :ssm_action for a value built from several columns, and (:choicecol, :rtcol) => combine => :ssm_action for what a function gives for their values. Used by column_map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.column_entry"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.column_map-Tuple{Union{Tuple, AbstractVector}, Any, Symbol}","page":"Fitting","text":"column_map(columns, cognitive_model, kind)\n\nReturn the columns of kind, :observations or :actions, as a NamedTuple from the name the model uses to the specification of the column, from what observation_columns or action_columns of MultipleSessionsInfo was given:\n\na tuple or a vector with an entry for each attribute, see column_entry: a name, for a column that is named as the model names the attribute, or a pair from the specification of the column to the model's name, such as :outcome => :reward;\none such pair on its own;\na single name, which is the column of the attribute of that kind when the model has only one in its attributes, whatever the column is called. It is an error when the model has several, and when the model's attributes have the name as one of the other kind only, since observation_columns and action_columns may then be swapped. When its attributes have none of that kind, it is a column of that name;\nhow to build a value from several columns, without the model's name: a NamedTuple of names, or a pair of a tuple of names and a function, for the attribute of that kind when the model has only one in its attributes.\n\nA specification is the name of a column, or how to build one from several, see columns_used. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.column_map"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.columns_used-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Fitting","text":"columns_used(specification)\n\nReturn the names of the columns of a table that one specification of an attribute's column uses, as a tuple. A specification is one of:\n\na Symbol, the column itself;\na NamedTuple of Symbols, such as (; choice = :choicecol, rt = :rtcol), for a column of NamedTuples with those names built from the columns, one for each row;\na Tuple of Symbols, such as (:choicecol, :rtcol), for a column of Tuples;\na Pair of a Tuple of Symbols and a function, such as (:choicecol, :rtcol) => (choice, rt) -> [choice, rt], for a column with what the function gives for the values of each row.\n\nUsed by MultipleSessionsInfo when it checks a table, and by build_column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.columns_used"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.data_names-Tuple{Any, Symbol}","page":"Fitting","text":"data_names(cognitive_model, kind)\n\nReturn the names in the attributes of cognitive_model, from attribute_names, for the data of kind, :observations or :actions: its inputs for the observations and its outputs for the actions. Used by column_map and named_data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.data_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.named_data-Tuple{Any, Any, Symbol}","page":"Fitting","text":"named_data(data, cognitive_model, kind)\n\nReturn the data of kind, :observations or :actions, as a NamedTuple of vectors: a single vector as the data of the attribute of that kind when the model has only one in its attributes, which is an error when it has another number of them, and anything else as it is, for check_data_fits to check. A vector of NamedTuples with the model's names is the data written as one row for each timestep, which is returned as it is, for check_data_fits to report. Used by SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.named_data"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.names_its_attribute-Tuple{Pair}","page":"Fitting","text":"names_its_attribute(column)\n\nReturn whether column, a pair given as the columns of a kind, ends in the name the model uses for the attribute, as in :outcome => :reward and (:choicecol, :rtcol) => combine => :ssm_action, and not in a function, as in (:choicecol, :rtcol) => combine. Used by column_map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.names_its_attribute"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.only_attribute_name-Tuple{Any, Symbol, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"only_attribute_name(cognitive_model, kind, columns)\n\nReturn the name of the attribute of kind, :observations or :actions, when the model has only one in its attributes, for columns, which build its value from several columns without naming it. It is an error when the model has another number of them, since the value then needs the model's name. Used by column_map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.only_attribute_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.rows_per_session-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Fitting","text":"rows_per_session(table, id_columns)\n\nReturn a vector with one pair for each session in table: its identifier, and the indices of its rows. A session is every row with one combination of the values in id_columns, in the order of the first row of each. The identifier is the value itself for a single column, and a NamedTuple of the values for several. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.rows_per_session"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.select_rows-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"select_rows(columns, rows)\n\nReturn the rows rows of each column in columns, a NamedTuple of vectors, which are the data of one session. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.select_timestep_sizes-Tuple{NamedTuple, Symbol, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"select_timestep_sizes(table, timestep_sizes, rows)\n\nReturn the sizes of the timesteps of one session: the column named timestep_sizes of table at the rows rows, or timestep_sizes itself when it is a size for all. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.session_id-Tuple{NamedTuple, Tuple{Symbol}, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"session_id(table, id_columns, i)\n\nReturn the identifier of the session that row i of table belongs to. Used by rows_per_session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.session_id"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.decide_whether_environment_is_included-Tuple{Union{Nothing, Bool}, Any, Any, Any, SaveHistory, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"decide_whether_environment_is_included(include_environment, environment, observations,\n                                       actions, environment_save_history,\n                                       check_stopping_criterion)\n\nReturn whether a session includes its environment, as the include_environment of SessionInfo says: always with true, which needs an environment, never with false, and with nothing only when an observation or an action is MissingValue, the environment's history is saved, or its stopping criterion is checked on every timestep. A session without an environment never includes one. With false, it is an error when an observation is MissingValue(), the environment's history is saved, or its stopping criterion is checked on every timestep, since those need the environment. Used by prepare_session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.decide_whether_environment_is_included"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.has_missing_entries-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"has_missing_entries(columns)\n\nReturn whether any entry of columns, the observations or the actions in the data of a session with one vector for each name, is MissingValue. Used by SessionInfo, to decide whether the environment runs, and by check_environment_fits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_missing_entries"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.include_environments_if_estimated-Tuple{Any, MultipleSessionsInfo}","page":"Fitting","text":"include_environments_if_estimated(environment_parameters_model, sessions_info)\n\nReturn the sessions as a full model runs them with environment_parameters_model: as they are when it estimates nothing, and otherwise with every session that has an environment including it, so that the observations in the data inform the parameters. Used by prepare_full_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.include_environments_if_estimated"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.remake_session_including_environment-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Fitting","text":"remake_session_including_environment(session_info)\n\nReturn the session including its environment when it has one, and everything else the same. A session that was prepared with include_environment = false is an error. Used by include_environments_if_estimated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.remake_session_including_environment"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.remake_session_without_environment-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Fitting","text":"remake_session_without_environment(session_info)\n\nReturn the session with NoEnvironment() as its environment and everything else the same, so that it runs as a session without an environment. Used by fitted sessions whose environment doesn't run, since every observation is in the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.remake_session_without_environment"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_unique_session_symbol_names-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_unique_session_symbol_names(session_ids)\n\nThrow an informative error if the identifiers of two sessions give the same symbol name, as session_id_to_symbol gives it: when they are the same identifier, or give the same text, such as 1 and \"1\". Used by MultipleSessionsInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_unique_session_symbol_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.group_sessions-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Tuple{}, String}","page":"Fitting","text":"group_sessions(sessions_info, group_by, reader)\n\nReturn the groups of the sessions in sessions_info by the values of the columns in group_by, a tuple of names: a NamedTuple with the group of every session, group_of_each_session, the number of groups, n_groups, and the symbol name of each group, group_symbol_names, made from its values as session_id_to_symbol makes the symbol name of a session from its identifier. Groups are numbered in the order of their first session, and with no columns every session is in one group. A session with more than one value, or a missing value, in one of the columns is an error, which starts with reader, as for read_one_value_of_each_session. Used by prepare_parameters_model, for the groups of a GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters and for the random intercepts of a HierarchicalRegression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.group_sessions"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.read_one_value_of_each_session-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo, Symbol, String}","page":"Fitting","text":"read_one_value_of_each_session(sessions_info, column, reader)\n\nReturn the value of column in every session of sessions_info, as a vector in the order of the sessions, from the table that the sessions were split from. A session with more than one value in the column, or with a missing one, is an error, which starts with reader, a description of what reads the column, such as \"GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters groups learning_rate by\". Used by group_sessions and by the regressions of a HierarchicalRegression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.read_one_value_of_each_session"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.check_environment_parameters-Tuple{SessionInfo{<:Any, <:CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}, NamedTuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"check_environment_parameters(session_info, environment_parameters)\ncheck_environment_parameters(sessions_info, environment_parameters)\n\nThrow an informative error if environment parameters are given for a session, or for sessions, without an environment model, which is decided by the types. Used by the models of several sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_environment_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.has_environment-Tuple{SessionInfo{<:Any, NoEnvironment}}","page":"Fitting","text":"has_environment(session_info)\nhas_environment(sessions_info)\n\nReturn whether a SessionInfo has an environment model, or every session of a MultipleSessionsInfo has, which is decided by the type. A session can have an environment which doesn't run, see SessionInfo. Used by check_environment_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_environment"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.last_timestep_with_data-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"last_timestep_with_data(columns)\n\nReturn the last timestep on which any column in columns, a NamedTuple of vectors, has a value that isn't MissingValue, and 0 when none has. Any other marker counts as data, since it says that the timestep happened. Used by first_timestep_to_check_stopping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.last_timestep_with_data"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.sessions_save_anything-Tuple{MultipleSessionsInfo}","page":"Fitting","text":"sessions_save_anything(sessions_info)\n\nReturn whether the save history of either model of any session in sessions_info selects anything. Used by the full models of the inference backends, to warn when the sessions save trajectories that nothing in the model uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.sessions_save_anything"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.build_named_entry-Union{Tuple{name}, Tuple{Val{name}, Any}} where name","page":"Fitting","text":"build_named_entry(::Val{name}, value)\n\nReturn (; name = value). A NamedTuple which holds a value that can have two types is built with merge of these, as select_row does. Used by select_row, by select_timestep_values_to_save and by the timesteps of fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.build_named_entry"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.has_missing_value-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"has_missing_value(data)\n\nReturn whether any entry of data, the NamedTuple of the actions or the observations of one timestep, is MissingValue. Used by the timesteps of fitted sessions, to skip imputing when nothing is missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_missing_value"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.select_row-Tuple{NamedTuple{()}, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"select_row(columns, t)\n\nReturn the NamedTuple of the values at timestep t of columns, a NamedTuple of vectors with one entry for each timestep, built with build_named_entry so that a column which holds two types, such as an action that is MissingValue on some timesteps, gives one of two NamedTuples with concrete fields rather than one without a definite type. Used by the timesteps of fitted sessions, and by the models of several sessions, which read the parameters of one session with it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_row"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.action_names_to_condition_on-Tuple{SessionInfo}","page":"Fitting","text":"action_names_to_condition_on(session_info)\nobservation_names_to_condition_on(session_info)\n\nReturn the names of the actions, or of the observations, that have values to condition on in the data of a session, which are scored against the distributions the cognitive model, or the environment model, gave for them, in a Val. A session without an environment scores no observations, so it has no such names. Used by fitted sessions, to select the distributions they score.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.action_names_to_condition_on"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.find_outputs_to_condition_on-Union{Tuple{names}, Tuple{NamedTuple{names}, NamedTuple, Any}} where names","page":"Fitting","text":"find_outputs_to_condition_on(data_output, output_distribution, model)\n\nReturn whether each output of model on one timestep has a value to condition on in the data, as a NamedTuple of Bools with the names of data_output: the actions of a cognitive model, or the observations an environment model gives. Such a value is scored against the distribution the model gave for it in output_distribution, so it is an error when the model gave nothing for it, NoValue. Used by the timesteps of fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.find_outputs_to_condition_on"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_output_distributions_for_conditioning-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"initialize_output_distributions_for_conditioning(distributions_for_conditioning)\n\nReturn an empty vector for the distributions of each output that has values to condition on in a session, typed by the distribution the model gave on the first timestep, so that the vectors keep one type through the session when every timestep gives the same type. An output the model doesn't give on the first timestep gets a Vector{Union{}}(), which the first distribution kept sets the type of, as for push_value!. Used by fitted sessions, after their first timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_output_distributions_for_conditioning"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.is_value_to_condition_on-Tuple{Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"is_value_to_condition_on(value)\n\nReturn whether a single action or observation in the data is a value to condition on: neither MissingValue nor an AbstractNoValue, such as UnimputedMissingValue or NoValue. Used by collect_values_to_condition_on and find_outputs_to_condition_on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.is_value_to_condition_on"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.store_output_distributions_for_conditioning-Union{Tuple{names}, Tuple{NamedTuple{names}, NamedTuple, NamedTuple}} where names","page":"Fitting","text":"store_output_distributions_for_conditioning(distributions,\n                                            distributions_for_conditioning,\n                                            outputs_to_condition_on)\n\nAdd the distributions of the outputs to condition on at one timestep, which outputs_to_condition_on says, to the vectors in distributions, one for each output that has values to condition on in the session, and return them. A distribution of another type than its vector widens the vector, as for push_value!. Used by fitted sessions, on every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_output_distributions_for_conditioning"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.select_cognitive_timestep_values_to_save-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"select_cognitive_timestep_values_to_save(cognitive_model, timestep_values, save_history)\nselect_environment_timestep_values_to_save(environment, environment_timestep_values,\n                                           save_history)\n\nReturn what save_history selects of one model's part of the values of a timestep: the states, observations, actions and action_distributions of the cognitive model, or the states, observations, actions and observation_distributions of the environment, from the values of the environment alone. The observation and the action can have one of two types, so they go in through build_named_entry. Used by select_timestep_values_to_save.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_cognitive_timestep_values_to_save"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.select_initial_timestep_values_to_save-Tuple{Val{true}, SessionInfo{<:Any, NoEnvironment}, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"select_initial_timestep_values_to_save(save_initial_timestep_values::Val, session_info,\n                                       previous)\n\nReturn what a session saves from before the first timestep, as timestep 0, when save_initial_timestep_values is Val(true), and nothing otherwise. previous has the values that the first timestep starts from, arranged as those of a timestep. The distributions of timestep 0 are BeforeFirstTimestep for every output of each model, since none was given yet. Used by fitted sessions, before their first timestep runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_initial_timestep_values_to_save"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.select_timestep_values_to_save-Tuple{SessionInfo{<:Any, NoEnvironment}, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"select_timestep_values_to_save(session_info, timestep_values)\n\nReturn what a session saves of the values of one timestep, or of where it starts, as the save histories of session_info select it, arranged as the session returns it: the cognitive model's states, observations, actions and action_distributions, and with an environment its states, observations, actions and observation_distributions under environment, as map_saved arranges them. timestep_values has the cognitive model's state, observation, action and action_distribution, and under environment the environment's state, observation, action and observation_distribution, each in its own names. Used by fitted sessions, on every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_timestep_values_to_save"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.timestep_sizes_until-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"timestep_sizes_until(timestep_sizes, n_saved)\n\nReturn the vector of the sizes of the timesteps that a session returns, cut to its first n_saved elements, for a session that ended before its data did when a stopping_criterion was met. An empty vector of sizes, for a session that saves nothing, is kept. Used by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.timestep_sizes_until"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.with_empty_environment_entry-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"with_empty_environment_entry(saved_trajectories)\n\nReturn saved_trajectories, what a session without an environment saved, with an environment entry whose vectors hold an empty NamedTuple for each saved timestep, for a session which has an environment but didn't include it, so that every session with an environment returns the same entries, in the same order, since the model's return otherwise has two types. Used by fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.with_empty_environment_entry"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.print_data_names-Tuple{IO, Any, Any}","page":"Fitting","text":"print_data_names(io, kind, names)\n\nPrint a line with kind and names, the names of one kind of value in the data, or none when there are none. Used by the show methods of a SessionInfo and a MultipleSessionsInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_data_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.print_model_name-Tuple{IO, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Fitting","text":"print_model_name(io, model)\n\nPrint the name of the type of model, or none for NoEnvironment. Used by the show methods of a SessionInfo and a MultipleSessionsInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_model_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/fitting/#CognitiveModeling.column_table-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Fitting","text":"column_table(table)\n\nReturn table as a NamedTuple with one vector for each column. A NamedTuple is returned as it is, and any other table is read with Tables.jl, through the package's extension for it, which loads with Tables.jl, as it does with DataFrames.jl, CSV.jl and the other packages of tables, since they load Tables.jl. Without it, a table that isn't a NamedTuple is an error. Used by MultipleSessionsInfo when it splits a table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.column_table"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#Models-from-POMDPs.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"POMDPs.jl describes a task as a problem object, and has solvers which give a policy for it, and updaters which keep a belief about the hidden state. A cognitive model can keep such a belief as its state, update it with their updater, and choose from the values their policy gives each action.\n\nThis page shows such a cognitive model. It doesn't simulate or fit it yet, and its code isn't run when the documentation is built, to keep the documentation's environment small. The package's tests run it instead, with POMDPs.jl 1.0, POMDPTools.jl 1.1 and QMDP.jl 0.1.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: DiscreteNonParametric\nusing LogExpFunctions: softmax\nusing POMDPs: POMDPs\nusing POMDPTools: DiscreteUpdater, actionvalues\nusing QMDP: QMDPSolver","title":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#A-Bayesian-observer-with-a-solved-policy","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"The cognitive model holds the problem, a policy for it and a belief updater. The problem is solved once, when the cognitive model is created, so nothing is solved on a timestep.\n\nstruct BayesianObserver{Tpomdp,Tpomdp_policy,Tpomdp_updater} <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    pomdp::Tpomdp\n    pomdp_policy::Tpomdp_policy\n    pomdp_updater::Tpomdp_updater\nend\n\nfunction BayesianObserver(pomdp)\n    return BayesianObserver(\n        pomdp, POMDPs.solve(QMDPSolver(), pomdp), DiscreteUpdater(pomdp)\n    )\nend\n\nIts state is the belief, which starts as the problem's distribution over initial states.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::BayesianObserver, parameters)\n    return (;\n        belief=POMDPs.initialize_belief(\n            model.pomdp_updater, POMDPs.initialstate(model.pomdp)\n        )\n    )\nend\n\nOn each timestep, the belief is updated from the previous action and the observation that followed it, and the action probabilities are a softmax over the values the policy gives each action under that belief, with the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl. On the first timestep there is no previous action, which AbstractNoValue covers, and the belief is left as it is.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(\n    model::BayesianObserver, context::ModelContext\n)\n\n    # Unpack the context ##\n    belief = context.previous_state.belief\n    previous_choice = context.previous_output.choice\n    β = context.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    # Update state ##\n    #Update the belief from the previous action and what was observed after it\n    if !(previous_choice isa AbstractNoValue)\n        belief = POMDPs.update(\n            model.pomdp_updater, belief, previous_choice, context.input.observation\n        )\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The value the policy gives each action under the belief\n    Qₜ = actionvalues(model.pomdp_policy, belief)\n    #Softmax over the action values, as a distribution over the problem's own actions\n    Pₜ = DiscreteNonParametric(collect(POMDPs.actions(model.pomdp)), softmax(β .* Qₜ))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice=Pₜ), (; belief))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::BayesianObserver)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters=:inverse_temperature,\n        states=:belief,\n        inputs=:observation,\n        outputs=:choice,\n    )\nend\n\nThe updater returns a new belief on each timestep, so the saved states need no copying.\n\nA problem from POMDPModels.jl gives a cognitive model for its task, such as BayesianObserver(TigerPOMDP()). The problem can be used as the environment too, through CommonRLEnvironment, as in CommonRLEnvironment(POMDPCommonRLEnv{Any}(TigerPOMDP()); action_name = :choice). The {Any} makes POMDPTools.jl give the observations as they are, which the updater needs. Without it, as with convert, each observation is put in an array, and the updater throws an error. That environment also gives reward and terminated, which aren't in this cognitive model's attributes, so a NameMap must select the observation for it: observation_map = NameMap(:observation).\n\nThe learners in TabularTDLearning.jl can't be used this way. They are solvers which run every episode themselves, so there is no single timestep to call.","title":"A Bayesian observer with a solved policy"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#Fitting","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"This page doesn't fit the cognitive model yet. When it is fitted with a sampler that uses gradients, use Enzyme.jl in reverse mode with runtime activity as the automatic differentiation backend, which for Turing.jl is AutoEnzyme(; mode = set_runtime_activity(Reverse), function_annotation = Const). It gave the right gradient of the log density of a session for this cognitive model, when checked against finite differences with Enzyme.jl 0.13. So did ForwardDiff.jl and ReverseDiff.jl, while Mooncake.jl and Enzyme's forward mode threw an error.\n\nUse the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl, as above, and not one written by hand. Written in one line, a softmax got a wrong gradient for this cognitive model from Enzyme's reverse mode, with no error, which the page Automatic differentiation and Reactant describes under its pitfalls.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"#CognitiveModeling.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia package for computational modeling of cognition and behavior. It is under development.","title":"CognitiveModeling.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Internals","page":"Overview","text":"These are only used inside the package, and can change without notice. What users work with is under API.\n\nDefining models\nSimulation\nSaving histories\nFitting\n\nPages = [\"defining_models.md\", \"simulation.md\", \"saving_histories.md\", \"fitting.md\"]","title":"Internals"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_common_rl/#Interfacing-with-CommonRLInterface.jl","page":"Interfacing with CommonRLInterface.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use an environment from CommonRLInterface.jl as the environment of a cognitive model, which also covers the environments of POMDPs.jl, through the wrappers of POMDPTools.jl, such as POMDPCommonRLEnv{Any}(pomdp), and those of ReinforcementLearning.jl, which convert to it.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Interfacing with CommonRLInterface.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Internals-of-defining-models","page":"Defining models","text":"Pages = [\"defining_models.md\"]","title":"Internals of defining models"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Types","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Utility-functions","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Utility functions"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Describing-a-model-in-messages","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Describing a model in messages"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Checking-a-model's-own-methods","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Checking a model's own methods"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Comparing-names","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Comparing names"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Checking-values-against-a-model's-names","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Checking values against a model's names"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Checking-parameters-against-their-domains","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Checking parameters against their domains"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Outputs-and-inputs-before-the-first-timestep","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Outputs and inputs before the first timestep"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/defining_models/#Displaying-models","page":"Defining models","text":"","title":"Displaying models"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel","page":"Defining models","text":"AbstractTimestepModel\n\nSupertype for timestep models: cognitive models and environment models, which every function that a model implements or is used with takes. A model is a subtype of AbstractCognitiveModel or of AbstractEnvironmentModel, and generally not of this type itself.\n\nThe two kinds work the same way, one timestep at a time: on each timestep a model receives an input and gives an output, in a ModelContext and a ModelOutput. In a simulation or a fitted session, the outputs of the cognitive model are its actions, which are the inputs of the environment model, and the outputs of the environment model are the observations, which are the inputs of the cognitive model. A cognitive model can therefore also serve as the environment of another.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.as_tuple-Tuple{Tuple}","page":"Defining models","text":"as_tuple(values)\n\nReturn values as a Tuple: a tuple as it is, a vector as a tuple, and anything else in a tuple of one. Used for the arguments that take one value or several: the names given to ModelAttributes, SaveHistory, MultipleSessionsInfo, GroupedHierarchicalSessionParameters and HierarchicalRegression, which are then asserted to be Symbols, and the values that stand for a missing value in SessionInfo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_tuple"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_unique_attribute_names-Tuple{ModelAttributes}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_unique_attribute_names(attributes)\n\nThrow an ArgumentError if a name in attributes, a ModelAttributes, is used for more than one attribute, within a kind or across the kinds, and return nothing otherwise. Whether the names are unique is decided by unique_names, and the message is only made when they aren't. Used by the constructor of ModelAttributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_unique_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.throw_duplicate_attribute_name-Tuple{ModelAttributes}","page":"Defining models","text":"throw_duplicate_attribute_name(attributes)\n\nThrow the ArgumentError of check_unique_attribute_names, naming the first name of attributes that is used twice and the kinds it is used for. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.throw_duplicate_attribute_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.unique_names-Tuple{Tuple{}}","page":"Defining models","text":"unique_names(names)\n\nReturn whether no name in the tuple names appears twice. It is unrolled over the names with has_name. Used by check_unique_attribute_names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.unique_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.describe_model-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"describe_model(model)\n\nReturn how model is named in an error message: as a cognitive model or as an environment model, with its type. Used in the error messages of the checks on models, in simulations and in fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.describe_model"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_model_output-Tuple{ModelOutput, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_model_output(output, model)\n\nReturn output if it is the ModelOutput that an apply_timestep_model method of model must return, and throw an error otherwise. Used on each timestep of simulate, simulate! and fitted sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_model_output"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_type_attribute_names-Tuple{ModelAttributes, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_type_attribute_names(attributes, model)\n\nReturn attributes if it is the ModelAttributes that a method of attribute_names must return, and throw an informative error otherwise. model is the cognitive model or the environment model whose method returned it. Used wherever the names of a model are read: by the checks of simulations, instantiated models, save histories and fitted sessions, and by the display of a model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_type_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.has_attribute_names-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"has_attribute_names(model)\n\nReturn whether model, a cognitive model or an environment model, has its own method of attribute_names, instead of the fallback which throws. Used by the display of a model, which shows one without a method of its own as not specified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.has_name-Tuple{Symbol, Tuple{}}","page":"Defining models","text":"has_name(name, names)\nhas_every_name(names, other_names)\n\nReturn whether name is among the tuple names, and whether every name in the tuple names is among other_names. Used by unique_names, and by the checks that a name map, a save history or the data has names in a model's attributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.same_names-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Defining models","text":"same_names(names, other_names)\n\nReturn whether two tuples of names have the same names, in any order. Used by the checks of names in simulations and fitted sessions, such as check_names_fit and check_match_attribute_names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.same_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_input_names-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_input_names(model, input)\n\nThrow an informative error if input, given to a model without the other side of a simulation, isn't a NamedTuple with every input in the attributes of model, from attribute_names. It can have more than those. Used by simulate and simulate! on a single input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_input_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_match_attribute_names-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_match_attribute_names(model, parameters, state, previous_output)\n\nThrow an informative error if the names of the parameters, of the previous output or of the state aren't those in the attributes of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, from attribute_names, or if the previous output or the state isn't a NamedTuple. Used by InstantiatedModel, simulate and fitted sessions, before anything runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_match_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_domains_fit_model-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, NamedTuple{()}}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_domains_fit_model(model, domains)\n\nThrow an ArgumentError if domains, what parameter_domains returns for model, names a parameter that isn't in its attributes, from attribute_names, or has a domain that isn't a NamedTuple with only lower, upper and discrete. Used by check_parameter_domains.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_domains_fit_model"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_each_parameter_domain-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, NamedTuple, NamedTuple{()}}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_each_parameter_domain(model, parameters, domains)\n\nCheck the value in parameters of each parameter with a domain in domains, one at a time, with check_value_in_domain. The names are read from the types, so that the values are checked without any search for them when the model runs. Used by check_parameter_domains.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_each_parameter_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_parameter_domains-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, NamedTuple}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_parameter_domains(model, parameters)\n\nThrow an InvalidParametersError if a parameter in parameters is outside its domain in parameter_domains of model: below lower, above upper, or not a whole number when it is discrete. Real numbers and arrays of them are checked, and anything else is left alone, such as the traced numbers of Reactant.jl, whose values can't be compared while a simulation is compiled. It is an ArgumentError when the domains name a parameter that isn't in the model's attributes, or have a field other than lower, upper and discrete. Used by simulate, InstantiatedModel and fitted sessions, before anything runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_parameter_domains"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.check_value_in_domain-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Symbol, Real, NamedTuple}","page":"Defining models","text":"check_value_in_domain(model, name, value, domain)\n\nThrow an InvalidParametersError if value, the value of the parameter name of model, is outside domain, a NamedTuple with any of lower, upper and discrete. Every element of an array of real numbers is checked, and a value that is neither is left alone. Used by check_each_parameter_domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_value_in_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.output_before_first_timestep-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"output_before_first_timestep(model)\ninput_before_first_timestep(model)\noutput_before_first_timestep(attributes)\ninput_before_first_timestep(attributes)\n\nReturn the output and the input from before the first timestep: a NamedTuple with the output or the input names in the attributes of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, from attribute_names, or those that attributes, a ModelAttributes, has, each BeforeFirstTimestep. A model's names are checked to be a ModelAttributes first. Used wherever a model starts: by InstantiatedModel, reset!, simulate and fitted sessions, for the previous output and for the output distributions saved as timestep 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.output_before_first_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.model_kind-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel}","page":"Defining models","text":"model_kind(model)\n\nReturn which kind of model model is, as a display names it: \"cognitive model\", \"environment model\", or \"model\" for one of neither kind. Used by the show methods of a model and of an InstantiatedModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.model_kind"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.print_attribute_names-Tuple{IO, ModelAttributes}","page":"Defining models","text":"print_attribute_names(io, attributes; shorten=false)\n\nPrint a line for each kind of attribute in attributes, a ModelAttributes, with none if it has no names of that kind. With shorten, a kind with more names than shows_every_name allows shows only how many there are.\n\nThe show methods of a ModelAttributes, of a cognitive model and of an environment model each print a header of their own, and then these lines. Only those of the models shorten them, since a ModelAttributes is where the full lists are read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.print_current_values-Tuple{IO, Any, NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Defining models","text":"print_current_values(io, label, current_values, field_name)\n\nPrint a line with label and current_values, the fields of a model or the parameters or the state of an InstantiatedModel, in a NamedTuple: each name with its value, none when it is empty, and only how many there are, with where to read them, when shows_every_name doesn't allow listing them all. Values are printed compactly, and large arrays are shortened in the REPL. Used by the show methods of a model and of an InstantiatedModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_current_values"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/defining_models/#CognitiveModeling.shows_every_name-Tuple{IO, Any}","page":"Defining models","text":"shows_every_name(io, names)\n\nReturn whether a display on io lists every one of names: always when io isn't limited, as when printing to a file, and otherwise when there are at most five. Used by the show methods of a model and of an InstantiatedModel, which give how many there are instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.shows_every_name"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Simulation","page":"Simulation","text":"Running a cognitive model, an environment model or the two together. What a simulation saves is chosen with a SaveHistory, on the page Saving histories.\n\nPages = [\"simulation.md\"]","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Simulating","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"Simulating"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Instantiated-models-and-name-maps","page":"Simulation","text":"","title":"Instantiated models and name maps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate!-Tuple{InstantiatedModel, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate!(instantiated_model::InstantiatedModel, input; timestep_size=1,\n          rng=default_rng())\n\nRun a single timestep of instantiated_model on input, update it, and return the sampled output. What instantiated_model.save_history selects of the state, the input, the output and the output distribution is added to instantiated_model.history, together with the size of the timestep.\n\nThe output is in a NamedTuple with the names the model gave it, as in output.choice, as for simulate. The input must be a NamedTuple with every input in the model's attributes, from attribute_names, or an ArgumentError is thrown.\n\nArguments\n\ninstantiated_model::InstantiatedModel: the model to update.\ninput::NamedTuple: what the model receives on this timestep, such as (; reward = 1.0) for a cognitive model. An entry that isn't there is NoValue.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nagent = InstantiatedModel(RescorlaWagner(), (; learning_rate = 0.5); save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()))\n\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\n\nagent.history.states\n\n# output\n\n3-element Vector{Any}:\n (expected_reward = 0.5,)\n (expected_reward = 0.75,)\n (expected_reward = 0.875,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate!-Tuple{InstantiatedModel, InstantiatedModel}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate!(agent::InstantiatedModel, environment::InstantiatedModel; <keyword arguments>)\n\nSimulate agent interacting with environment from where they are, update them both, and return the trajectories of this call in the same NamedTuple as simulate, without timestep 0: what agent.save_history selects of the states, observations, actions and action distributions on each timestep, what environment.save_history selects of the environment's under environment, and the sizes of the timesteps. What the two select is also added to agent.history and environment.history, in each model's own names, together with the sizes of the timesteps, unless a selection is empty. A history has timestep 0 from where the model started, when its SaveHistory saves the initial timestep values.\n\nThe two models must fit together, as for simulate, directly or through observation_map and action_map. The agent's history holds the observations as its model received them, and the environment's history the actions as its model did.\n\nThe simulation stops before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of either model is met, unless check_stopping_criterion is false. A model where it is met already can't be simulated further, and throws an error until it is reset!.\n\nAn instantiated model isn't type stable, as InstantiatedModel describes. Use simulate where that matters, or if most calls are for a single timestep.\n\nArguments\n\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: who moves first on each timestep.\nn_steps::Int: the number of timesteps to simulate, which must be at least 1. It is the most timesteps there can be when a model has a stopping_criterion.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers. A value that isn't a vector, such as a single number, is the size of every timestep of this call. A vector has one size for each timestep of this call, so its length must be n_steps.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\nobservation_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which observations of the environment the agent receives, and under which names, as for simulate.\naction_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which actions of the agent the environment receives, and under which names, as for simulate.\ncheck_stopping_criterion::Bool=true: whether stopping_criterion of the two models is checked before each timestep, as for simulate.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RandomChoices <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RandomChoices, parameters)\n    return (;)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(model::RandomChoices, context::ModelContext)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.5)))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RandomChoices)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = (), inputs = :timestep, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nstruct TimestepCounter <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TimestepCounter, parameters)\n    return (; n_timesteps = 0)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(model::TimestepCounter, context::ModelContext)\n    n_timesteps = context.previous_state.n_timesteps + 1\n    return ModelOutput((; timestep = Deterministic(n_timesteps)), (; n_timesteps))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TimestepCounter)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = :n_timesteps, inputs = :choice, outputs = :timestep)\nend\n\nagent = InstantiatedModel(RandomChoices(), (;))\nenvironment = InstantiatedModel(TimestepCounter(), (;))\n\nsimulate!(agent, environment; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), n_steps = 2)\nsimulate!(agent, environment; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), n_steps = 3)\n\nenvironment.state\n\n# output\n\n(n_timesteps = 5,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, Any}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate(model, input; <keyword arguments>)\n\nRun a single timestep of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, on input, without the other side of a simulation, and return the sampled output and the new state of the model.\n\nNothing is kept between calls, so pass the returned state and output on as previous_state and previous_output to run the next timestep. simulate! on a single input does the same for an InstantiatedModel, which keeps them. This is type stable, which an instantiated model isn't, so it is the faster of the two.\n\nThe output is in a NamedTuple with the names the model gave it, as in output.choice. The input must be a NamedTuple with every input in the model's attributes, from attribute_names, or an ArgumentError is thrown. Parameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if model provides defaults for them, and the names of the parameters, the state and the previous output are checked as in a full simulation.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel::AbstractTimestepModel: the model.\ninput::NamedTuple: what the model receives on this timestep, such as (; reward = 1.0) for a cognitive model. An entry that isn't there is NoValue.\nparameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\nprevious_state=initialize_state(model, ...): the state of model before this timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters. A state that model changes in place will be changed.\nprevious_output=output_before_first_timestep(model): the output before this timestep. By default every output is BeforeFirstTimestep.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nparameters = (; learning_rate = 0.5)\n\n#The first timestep starts from the initial state\naction, state = simulate(RescorlaWagner(), (; reward = 1.0); parameters)\n\n#The next one continues from what the first returned\naction, state = simulate(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    (; reward = 1.0);\n    parameters,\n    previous_state = state,\n    previous_output = action,\n)\n\nstate\n\n# output\n\n(expected_reward = 0.875,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.simulate-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel, CognitiveModeling.AbstractTimestepModel}","page":"Simulation","text":"simulate(cognitive_model, environment; <keyword arguments>)\n\nSimulate cognitive_model interacting with environment, and return the trajectories that were saved, which are what changed over the timesteps, in a NamedTuple: states, observations, actions and action_distributions of the cognitive model, environment with the states, observations, actions and observation_distributions of the environment, and timestep_sizes. Each is a vector with one element for timestep 0, before the first timestep, and one for each timestep after it.\n\nEach element is what happened on that timestep, and the states are those after it. The observations are those the cognitive model received, and the actions those it gave, and under environment the observations are those the environment gave and the actions those it received, which differ from the cognitive model's when a NameMap selects or renames them. The action distributions are what the cognitive model gave its actions to be sampled from, and the observation distributions what the environment gave its observations to be sampled from. The element for timestep 0 has the previous observation and action, which are BeforeFirstTimestep unless others are given, BeforeFirstTimestep() for every distribution, and the states before the first timestep. Since Julia counts from 1, element t + 1 is timestep t.\n\ncognitive_save_history and environment_save_history select what is kept of each element of each model, which is nothing by default: give SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) to keep everything, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything(); save_initial_timestep_values = false) to leave out timestep 0, so that element t is timestep t. The vectors of both models have the same length, which cognitive_save_history decides. The sizes of the timesteps are returned as they were given: a single size as it is, and a vector of sizes with the sizes of the timesteps that ran, after BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0 when it is there.\n\nParameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if cognitive_model or environment provides defaults for them. The names of the parameters, the states, the previous action and the previous observation must be those in attribute_names, and the outputs of each model must be the inputs of the other, or an ArgumentError is thrown before the first timestep. When the two name things differently, or one gives something that isn't in the other's attributes, observation_map and action_map say which outputs of one the other receives, and under which names.\n\nThe simulation stops before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of either model is met, unless check_stopping_criterion is false, and everything returned then has the timesteps that ran.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractTimestepModel: the cognitive model, usually an AbstractCognitiveModel.\nenvironment::AbstractTimestepModel: the environment model, usually an AbstractEnvironmentModel. A cognitive model can serve as the environment of another when its outputs are the inputs in the other's attributes, and vice versa.\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: who moves first on each timestep. It has no default, because a wrong choice shifts the model by a timestep without any error.\nn_steps::Int: the number of timesteps to simulate, which must be at least 1. It is the most timesteps there can be when a model has a stopping_criterion.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, which both cognitive_model and environment receive. A value that isn't a vector, such as a single number, is the size of every timestep. A vector has one size for each timestep, so its length must be n_steps.\ncognitive_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of cognitive_model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\nenvironment_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of environment, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample actions and observations from, which cognitive_model and environment also receive in their contexts.\ncognitive_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): which states, inputs, outputs and output distributions of cognitive_model are saved, by name, and whether timestep 0 is. Nothing is saved by default, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. See SaveHistory.\nenvironment_save_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): the same for environment, whose inputs are the actions it receives and whose outputs are the observations it gives. Whether timestep 0 is saved follows cognitive_save_history, so that the vectors of both models have the same length.\nobservation_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which observations of environment are passed on to cognitive_model, and under which names, as a NameMap from the names environment gives to exactly those in the attributes of cognitive_model. Without one, the two must have the same observations in their attributes, which cognitive_model receives under the same names.\naction_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which actions of cognitive_model are passed on to environment, and under which names, as a NameMap from the names cognitive_model gives to exactly those in the attributes of environment. Without one, the two must have the same actions in their attributes, which environment receives under the same names.\ncheck_stopping_criterion::Bool=true: whether stopping_criterion of the two models is checked before each timestep. With false, all of n_steps timesteps run.\nprevious_cognitive_state=initialize_state(cognitive_model, ...): the state of cognitive_model before the first timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters. A state that cognitive_model changes in place will be changed.\nprevious_action=output_before_first_timestep(cognitive_model): the action before the first timestep. By default every action is BeforeFirstTimestep, with the names from attribute_names.\nprevious_environment_state=initialize_state(environment, ...): the state of environment before the first timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters.\nprevious_observation=output_before_first_timestep(environment): the observation before the first timestep. By default every observation is BeforeFirstTimestep(), with the names from attribute_names of environment.\n\nSee also simulate!, which continues from where the last simulation left off.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli, BernoulliLogit\n\n#A cognitive model which learns the expected reward\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = BernoulliLogit(Vₜ - 0.5)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\n#An environment which takes the choice, and gives a reward with a fixed probability whatever it is\nstruct BernoulliRewards <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(environment::BernoulliRewards, parameters)\n    return (;)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(environment::BernoulliRewards, context::ModelContext)\n    return ModelOutput((; reward = Bernoulli(context.parameters.reward_probability)))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::BernoulliRewards)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :reward_probability, states = (), inputs = :choice, outputs = :reward)\nend\n\nsimulation = simulate(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    BernoulliRewards();\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps = 3,\n    cognitive_parameters = (; learning_rate = 0.5),\n    environment_parameters = (; reward_probability = 1.0),\n    cognitive_save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()),\n)\n\n#The initial state, as timestep 0, and the state after each timestep\nsimulation.states\n\n# output\n\n4-element Vector{@NamedTuple{expected_reward::Float64}}:\n (expected_reward = 0.5,)\n (expected_reward = 0.75,)\n (expected_reward = 0.875,)\n (expected_reward = 0.9375,)\n\nExtended help\n\nThe return type is concrete as long as the types of the outputs don't change between timesteps. If they do, like an observation with an entry that is sometimes NoValue, the vector for that output holds a Union of the types. The same goes for timestep 0 when it has other types than the timesteps after it, which the observations and actions usually do, since they are BeforeFirstTimestep() there.\n\nNothing is copied when outputs are saved. A model or environment which changes an object in its state in place, instead of returning a new one, must add a method of prepare_state_for_saving, or all the saved states show the final values, with no error. Observations and actions have no such function, so they must be new objects on each timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.InstantiatedModel","page":"Simulation","text":"InstantiatedModel(model, parameters; save_history=SaveHistory())\nInstantiatedModel(model, parameters, state, previous_output; save_history=SaveHistory())\n\nA cognitive model or an environment model with its parameters, current state and previous output, which simulate! can update iteratively.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as agent.state. Parameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if the model provides defaults for them. The state defaults to the output of initialize_state with the full parameters, and the previous output to every output being BeforeFirstTimestep. The previous output and the state must be NamedTuples, and the names of the parameters, of the previous output and of the state must be those in the model's attributes, from attribute_names, or an error is thrown.\n\nagent.history holds a ModelHistory with where the model started, which is timestep 0, and everything that happens to it, as selected by save_history, a SaveHistory. By default nothing is selected and the vectors in agent.history stay empty, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. States, inputs and outputs can only be selected by name among those in the model's attributes, since the history starts from those names. agent.n_timesteps counts the timesteps the model has simulated, whatever is saved.\n\nIn the REPL, an instantiated model shows the kind and the type of its model, how many timesteps it has simulated, and the values of its parameters and of its state, with only how many there are when there are more than five. Large arrays are shortened. When the model's stopping_criterion is met, it says so, since it must then be reset! before it can be simulated again. On one line, as inside a vector, it shows the type and the kind of its model and how many timesteps it has simulated.\n\nAn instantiated model is a convenience, and isn't type stable, by design: its state and its previous output can change type, as an output does from BeforeFirstTimestep() to a Bool, and so can what its history saves, so they are kept untyped. Each call of simulate! on it is therefore dynamically dispatched, and the compiler doesn't know what it returns, whatever is saved. Where that matters, use simulate on the models themselves, one timestep at a time or for a whole simulation.\n\nThe same type serves as the agent and as the environment of a simulation, since both are models. See also simulate! and reset!.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct LearnsRewards <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(::LearnsRewards, parameters) = (; expected_reward = 0.5);\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(::LearnsRewards, context::ModelContext)\n           V = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n           V = V + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V)\n           return ModelOutput((; choice = Deterministic(V > 0.5)), (; expected_reward = V))\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(::LearnsRewards) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice);\n\njulia> agent = InstantiatedModel(LearnsRewards(), (; learning_rate = 0.5));\n\njulia> simulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0));\n\njulia> agent\n-- Instantiated cognitive model --\nType: LearnsRewards\nTimesteps simulated: 1\nParameters: learning_rate = 0.5\nState: expected_reward = 0.75\n\njulia> print(agent)\nLearnsRewards (instantiated cognitive model, 1 timestep simulated)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.InstantiatedModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.ModelHistory","page":"Simulation","text":"ModelHistory(states, inputs, outputs, output_distributions, timestep_sizes)\n\nWhat has happened to an InstantiatedModel, which simulate! adds to.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as history.states. The names are the model's own: for a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it received and the outputs the observations it gave. The model's SaveHistory selects what is kept of each kind, and when it selects nothing at all, the vectors stay empty. Each vector starts with timestep 0, where the model started, when the SaveHistory saves the initial timestep values, which it does by default, so that element t + 1 is timestep t.\n\nThe vectors hold values of any type, since what is saved can change type from one timestep to the next, as an output does from BeforeFirstTimestep() at timestep 0 to a value.\n\nArguments\n\nstates::Vector{Any}: the states of the model, starting at timestep 0 with its initial state. Later states are those after each timestep. All of them are as returned by prepare_state_for_saving.\ninputs::Vector{Any}: what the model received on each timestep, starting at timestep 0 with every input in the model's attributes as BeforeFirstTimestep, since it has received nothing before the simulation.\noutputs::Vector{Any}: what the model gave on each timestep, starting at timestep 0 with its previous output, which by default has every output as BeforeFirstTimestep().\noutput_distributions::Vector{Any}: what the model gave each output to be sampled from, starting at timestep 0 with BeforeFirstTimestep() for every output, since nothing was given yet.\ntimestep_sizes::Vector{Any}: how much time each timestep covered, starting with BeforeFirstTimestep() for timestep 0, so that it has an entry for each entry of the other vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelHistory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.NameMap","page":"Simulation","text":"NameMap(names...)\n\nWhich outputs of one side of a simulation the other side receives, and under which names.\n\nEach argument is :given => :received, for an output which has another name in the receiver's attributes, or a single Symbol, for one which keeps its name. Outputs that aren't in the map aren't passed on, and each name can only be there once.\n\nsimulate and simulate! take one as observation_map, from the observations the environment model gives to those in the cognitive model's attributes, and one as action_map, from the actions the cognitive model gives to those in the environment model's attributes. A cognitive model and an environment which name things differently, or where one gives something that isn't in the other's attributes, can then be used together, and the map says which outputs the receiver gets: it must give exactly the names in the receiver's attributes, from attribute_names. NameMap() passes nothing on, for a receiver without inputs in its attributes. Without a map, the two must have the same names in their attributes.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> NameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue)\nNameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue)\n\njulia> NameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue => :reward)\nERROR: ArgumentError: A NameMap can only give each name once, but it gives (:reward, :reward).\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NameMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/simulation/#CognitiveModeling.reset!-Tuple{InstantiatedModel}","page":"Simulation","text":"reset!(instantiated_model::InstantiatedModel)\n\nPut instantiated_model back to where a new one starts, and return it.\n\nThe state becomes the output of initialize_state with the parameters it has, the previous output has every output as BeforeFirstTimestep again, and n_timesteps is 0. The history is a new one which starts from there, as timestep 0 when the SaveHistory saves the initial timestep values, so a history that was read before the reset, as in history = agent.history, is kept as it was. The model, the parameters and the SaveHistory stay the same.\n\nAn instantiated model that was created with a state or a previous output of its own doesn't return to those. Create a new one for that.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_timestep_model(model::RescorlaWagner, context::ModelContext)\n    V₋₁ = context.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + context.parameters.learning_rate * (context.input.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, inputs = :reward, outputs = :choice)\nend\n\nagent = InstantiatedModel(RescorlaWagner(), (; learning_rate = 0.5); save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()))\n\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nreset!(agent)\n\nagent.state, agent.previous_output, agent.history.states\n\n# output\n\n((expected_reward = 0.5,), (choice = BeforeFirstTimestep(),), Any[(expected_reward = 0.5,)])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.reset!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/saving_histories/#Saving-histories","page":"Saving histories","text":"What a simulation or a fitted session saves of each model, chosen by name, and whether it saves where the models started, as timestep 0.\n\nPages = [\"saving_histories.md\"]","title":"Saving histories"},{"category":"type","location":"api/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.SaveEverything","page":"Saving histories","text":"SaveEverything()\n\nMarker for saving every attribute of a kind, used in SaveHistory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SaveEverything"},{"category":"type","location":"api/saving_histories/#CognitiveModeling.SaveHistory","page":"Saving histories","text":"SaveHistory(; states=(), inputs=(), outputs=(), output_distributions=(),\n            save_initial_timestep_values=true)\nSaveHistory(SaveEverything(); save_initial_timestep_values=true)\n\nWhich states, inputs, outputs and output distributions of a model a simulation or a fitted session saves, by name, and whether it saves them from before the first timestep too.\n\nstates, inputs, outputs and output_distributions are each the names of that kind to save, in a Tuple of Symbols, a single Symbol or a Vector of them, or SaveEverything for all of them. The names are those in the model's attributes, from attribute_names: for a cognitive model the inputs are its observations and the outputs its actions, and for an environment model the inputs are the actions it receives and the outputs the observations it gives. The output distributions are what the model gave its outputs to be sampled from, named like the outputs. A kind that isn't given saves nothing, so SaveHistory() saves nothing, which is the default everywhere, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. The selections are read as properties, such as save_history.states.\n\nsave_initial_timestep_values is whether what is saved starts with timestep 0, before the first timestep: the initial states, and the previous input and output, which are BeforeFirstTimestep unless others are given. It is true by default, which matters when the initial state depends on parameters that are fitted. Since Julia counts from 1, element t + 1 of what is saved is then timestep t, and with false, element t is.\n\nSaving some names of a kind gives a NamedTuple with those names on each timestep, and saving none gives an empty one, (;). Before the first timestep there is no output distribution, so timestep 0 has BeforeFirstTimestep() for each name that is saved.\n\nEach model of a simulation has one: simulate and SessionInfo take cognitive_save_history and environment_save_history, and an InstantiatedModel takes save_history. The trajectories that a simulation or a session returns are named by their role: the cognitive model's inputs as observations and outputs as actions, and the environment's inputs as actions and outputs as observations under environment, as simulate describes.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> save_history = SaveHistory(; outputs = :choice, states = (:expected_reward, :surprise))\nSaveHistory\nStates: expected_reward, surprise\nInputs: none\nOutputs: choice\nOutput distributions: none\nInitial timestep values: saved\n\njulia> save_history.states\n(:expected_reward, :surprise)\n\njulia> SaveHistory(SaveEverything()).inputs\nSaveEverything()\n\njulia> SaveHistory(SaveEverything(); save_initial_timestep_values = false).save_initial_timestep_values\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SaveHistory"}]
}
